        READ_TEXT_2000047 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Recipient: <H>Rhythm Fischer</>, Icelake Escort, 6th Legion, Cocytus Region

For demonstrating decisive leadership, exemplary bravery, and exceptional operational efficiency in multiple <H>Filthoid-clearing</> campaigns across the years.
For her irreplaceable role in upholding public order and safeguarding the people of Icelake.

Upon the assessment of the Legion, and by the decree of His Majesty Julius, Rhythm Fischer is hereby awarded the Emperor's Signet Ring, and formally promoted to the rank of Warrant Officer. Let her serve as an inspiration to all soldiers, urging them to strive ever higher for the Empire's glory.

All glory to our Emperor.]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000047"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000048 = {
          ContentEN = "<H>Deteriorators Are Exceptionally Dangerous — Do Not Be Fooled by Their Appearance!</>\nOn September 24th, an egregious case of Deterioration occurred in Icelake, with devastating consequences. Following an comprehensive investigation by the 6th Legion's Escort Unit, the details of the tragedy have now been confirmed:\nOn September 13th, local residents Mr and Mrs Fischer encountered a gravely injured young man, Robie Brown, collapsed by the roadside near Lamenting Lake. Acting out of compassion, they brought him into their home and cared for him.\nHowever, Robie Brown was a Deteriorator — one who had evaded the Empire's routine screenings and fled to the outskirts of the city. While receiving aid from the Fischers, Brown deliberately concealed his condition. Ten days later, he succumbed to the transformation, becoming a Filthoid. The resulting massacre in Icelake's southern district claimed the lives of dozens, including Mr and Mrs Fischer. The 6th Legion's Escort responded swiftly and succeeded in subduing the threat — but not before the damage was done.\n\nThe incident resulted in 18 deaths (including 3 Solarian citizens) and 34 injuries. Citizens are reminded: under no circumstances should you provide shelter to unidentified Charon individuals. If you suspect someone of being a Deteriorator, report them to the Security Bureau immediately. Deteriorators are — and will remain — humanity's enemies. No exceptions. Harbouring Deteriorators or concealing their condition is a capital offence under Article 54 of the Hyperborean Criminal Code, punishable by death.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000048"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000049 = {
          ContentEN = "Brawl at Galea Theatre\n\nDate: October 9th, 8:30 PM\n\nFiled by: Letizia Putsch, Director of the Magic Flute Troupe\n\nInvolved Parties: Parsifal Snowfield (Lead Performer), Susanna Rossie (Lead Performer), Mathew Bernard (Audience), Feeley Francisco (Audience)\n...\n\nIncident Summary: A physical altercation reportedly broke out between troupe members and two front-row audience members. Alleged assault of performers by said audience members. (Several pages of the incident report appear to have been torn out.)\n\nConclusion: Evidence found to be insufficient. Case dismissed.\n\nRemarks: Performances of Children From the Snowfield have been suspended until further notice. No resumption date announced.\nI'd hoped to watch this one with Blues — to finally make up for the show we missed all those years ago. But I guess... that won't be happening any time soon.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000049"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000050 = {
          ContentEN = "...\nFinally put all the stuff Rhythm stored away back where it belongs. Come on — a secret base is supposed to be messy! If we follow her neat-freak rules, this whole place will end up looking like some boring museum.\nWhen will my fussy big sis finally get it? The real thrill's stumbling on long-lost treasure hidden under clutter one day!\n...\nPassed the 6th Legion's entrance test — yay! And my request to officially use this abandoned maintenance room got approved too — double yay!\nCan't believe the higher-ups said yes — just like that. No more sneaking in and out. Nice.\n...\nGave the key to Rhythm today. We've already made a plan: once I get back from the 6th Legion, we're gonna make this place bigger. One room just for my tapes, with the biggest stereo, loudest mic, and the best harmonica money can buy! No real performer skimps on gear!\nAnother room for the posters I copied, my paints, sketchboards, and stuff. Aside from acting, I think I've actually got a bit of talent at drawing... Maybe a stage-prop designer isn't such a bad career choice either? Whatever — I'll just try everything whenever I get the chance!\nOh, and definitely a room just for our medals. A few years from now, I'm sure we'll both be Laurelled Charons with medals piled high. Gotta reserve an entire room just to display them properly.\nAnd I also want to—! ...Ah. Looks like this journal's full already. Guess I'll have to pick up another one once I reach the inner city.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000050"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000051 = {
          ContentEN = "Chapter 235 – My Heart Belongs to Hyperborea Alone\n\nThe celebration banquet had ended. Most had drifted into well-earned sleep — save for the soldiers still out on patrol. Quietly, the adjutant moved between tents, careful not to disturb her comrades' rare moment of rest.\nAt last, she lifted the flap of one familiar tent. Inside sat a composed figure that everyone relied on — methodically loading bullets into a magazine, one round at a time.\n\"Chief, we've already pulled back from the front. You don't have to stay on edge like this.\"\n\"When's the next battle?\"\nLeo spoke without even looking up, as if he hadn't heard her well-meaning concern.\n\"You crushed the Elysian forces in the last engagement. They're unlikely to move again anytime soon.\"\nLeo said nothing. A tense silence hung between them. Sweat trickled down the adjutant's brow as she hesitated. Then, biting her lip, she reached into her coat and pulled out a bundle of ornate letters.\n\"Chief... can't you take a moment to enjoy life like everyone else — just for once? Look — here's an invitation to the Duchess's ball, one from the Princess's hunting party, and another—\"\n\"'I am either engaged in pressing military affairs or occupied with guarding against infiltration from Elysium.' Use the same reply for all.\"\n\"But... that's the same excuse you've used eighteen times now...\"\nClack—\nLeo set the gun aside and stepped toward the banner of the Three Wolves hanging at the rear of the tent. His hand reached out, brushing the scorched edges — still bearing the bitter stain of battle.\n\"I understand your concern. And I appreciate their courtesy. But while we rest, Elysian raiders still strike at our borders, driving countless Solarians from their homes. They march beneath the banner of false gods, crying out for a purge of heresy — nothing but a pretext for butchering our comrades and kin. But we know the truth: <H>we are not heretics — only those who have broken free from the chains of false gods. Those who still yearn — for freedom, and for the right to reclaim it.</>\"\nHis fist clenched. The banner bunched in his grip.\n\"Their so-called gods still squat atop our homeland. Until their divine tower falls, my heart will know no peace. If ever I am to enjoy life... let it be when I am shrouded in this flag after death, so that I may kiss it even—\"\n\"Please don't say things like that!\"\nShe cut him off. Leo looked up in surprise — but this time, she didn't avert her eyes. She met his gaze, steady and unwavering.\n\"Chief, I...\"\n\n\nEven the invincible Leo must one day face the battlefield of... social engagements!? What is it that his loyal adjutant truly wishes to say? \nThe saga continues in the next issue of The Invincible Legion's Chief!",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000051"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000052 = {
          ContentEN = "...\nAccording to the sewer layout, they must've fled into this sector. Worse than I thought: our specialised scanners can't get a proper reading down here. Looks like I'll be stuck in this sunless maze for days...\n...\nStill no sign of them today. Nearly ran into the Escort, though. Could the Legion already be onto this? I need to get in touch with the Professor as soon as possible...\n...\nEven with everything unfolding, the Professor remains as composed as ever. I still have much to learn from him. When we meet again in the sewers tomorrow, I need to hold it together — can't let him see me as a mess again.\n...",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000052"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000053 = {
          ContentEN = "Act Ⅰ – Nocturne of the Swans\n\nThe tale opens during the reign of Yushino, third Grand Duke of Yashnia. Long before he inherited the title, Yushino was famed as a patron of the arts — immersed in painting, court verse, and letters. He maintained a grand troupe of dancers, each handpicked by the duke himself. Word of the troupe's brilliance spread so widely that nobles from both the Empire's capital and the Church's seat contrived to serve as envoys, if only to witness one performance in that seaside duchy.\n\nYet when our curtain rises, Yushino does not praise art or boast of his refined taste. Instead he broods over the bane of his sleepless nights: too many siblings. His father, the second Grand Duke, sired more than a hundred sons, and every one of those brothers now covets the throne of so small a state. Whenever Yushino closes his eyes, their faces float before him. He once tried counting them to lull himself to sleep, only to grow more agitated. On the tenth sleepless night, he made a decision.\n\nHe spent ever more time at banquets, drinking heavily in hope of a few hours' oblivion — meagre, but better than none.\nWhen courtiers counselled restraint he waved them off, declaring: \"If anything in this world can grant me rest — be it at a molten mountain's core, in a whirlpool at sea, or flickering on the rim of the sky — I shall possess it.\"\n\nThus, in the first year of his rule, from sunrise to moonset he held feast after feast, inviting his brothers to dine beside him. Pomegranate‑coloured wine never ceased to flow, nor did the dark veins in his bloodshot eyes fade. Beneath the crystal dome, youths in scarlet gauze danced the Swan Dance and sang without pause. Their floating sleeves swept across the nobles' cheeks, leaving them flushed — like clouds blushed by the sun before it sinks beneath the waves.\nAnd each time, the Grand Duke would raise his goblet, wine running into his beard, his smile twisting into something grotesque. Then, catching his brothers in that hazy, entranced moment, he would murmur: \"Would you care to bring one of these sweet-voiced swans home, dear brother?\" And under the spell of wine and swirling dancers — who could refuse such largesse?\n\nThus a thousand swans flew out, one by one, into noble households.\n\nParsifal's Note:\nIn this sequence the ensemble dance is the centrepiece. Momiji appears here as one among many nameless swans. She must catch the audience's eye — but never so brightly as to break the unity of the dance. That balance is the true challenge.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000053"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000054 = {
          ContentEN = "Act Ⅱ – Bloodred Meteor Shower\n\nIn the third year of Grand Duke Yushino's reign, nightly revels were no longer confined to his own palace. Every one of his noble brothers had fallen under the spell of Yashnia's sweet‑voiced， graceful swans. Their songs drifted beyond the high walls of noble mansions; even the ragged street children could hum a line or two.\nTo mark the third anniversary of his ascension, Yushino proposed that these long‑scattered swans be gathered at the celebration beneath the palace dome for a single chorus.\nThe courtiers urged caution. The dancers had not sung together in years, they warned; if their harmony faltered before so many guests, ridicule was certain.\nBut Yushino — half‑swallowed by his gilded throne — would not be swayed. In his mind, the voices of the swans and the pulse of his own heart were still one.\n\nThe celebration began as scheduled. Nobles filled the hall. The swan dancers reunited in the centre of the marble floor. Their faces betrayed no joy, no sorrow — for they were swans, trained to sway with the music, to shape their smiles to the tune.\nAt a single, crystal note from Yushino's favourite dancer, a thousand voices rose as one — clear and keen, like lightning splitting the sky. When the last chord fell away, silence descended — like the sea after a storm, so calm it seemed the revelry moments ago had been no more than a dream.\nYushino's brothers drifted into deep, sugared dreams. He watched, envying their peace. Since taking the crown he himself had never slept so soundly.\n\nBut tonight — beneath the bright moonlight — surely even he could find peace.\n\n\"Tatarnaga. Four hundred and ninety‑nine insults against the Grand Duke. Verdict: execution.\"\nThe voice from the throne was ice. One by one, Yushino pronounced the name of the noble who had scorned him most — and the long list of charges, meticulously recorded by the very swan dancer he had gifted Tatarnaga three years before.\nThe dancer who had long kept close company with Tatarnaga stepped forward, her serenity unchanged. A gauzy sleeve brushed the nobleman's cheek, just as on the night they first met. Then — a dull thud. His head, still wearing the soft curve of an intoxicated smile, parted from his body before fear could reach his eyes. It sailed cleanly through the air, trailing a fine red arc — a meteor of flesh and blood. Scarlet rain spattered down, staining the swans' pristine robes.\nThe night was young; the stars were already falling.\n\nYushino barely glanced at the carnage. He seized the hair of the courtier who had doubted the swans' unity and snarled: \"Their loyalty has never broken pitch. Fool. Drink — and be as obedient as they are!\"\nBlood‑dark wine flooded the man's mouth and nose. He choked, certain he would drown.\nThen — to his astonishment — Yushino's grip slackened. The Grand Duke sagged back, and a thunderous snore rolled through the marble hall.\nNever had such a sound echoed in that palace, yet from that night onward it returned at the same hour every evening.\n\n\nParsifal's Note:\nThis scene calls for a solo. Traditionally, Momiji's sung by a soprano — but I've seen tenors pull it off brilliantly. I'd very much like to try it.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000054"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000055 = {
          ContentEN = "Act Ⅲ – A Thousand Letters\n\n\"To my most revered, most tenderly pitied, most admired Grand Duke Yushino — you who once spent three sleepless years for the continuance of Yashnia.\nThis is the one thousand and first secret letter I have written to you — in loyalty, and in love. And as in every letter before, I write what I shall gladly write again, and again, and again:\n\nI am forever your docile swan.\nI am forever, forever yours.\nForever and ever and ever.\n\nI imagine that, by the time you read this, a smile must have softened your weary face. One of the courtiers kneeling below your throne would stir at the sound of your laughter and ask what has pleased you so. You'd ignore him, drink half a glass of Hyperborean wine — if you drained it all, it would spill through your beard and stain your robe.\nYou'd sip while you read, deciding whether to finish the drink in peace... or smash it in rage.\n\nYes, I know you. Your face is etched into my heart and soul.\n\nFrom the day you sent me to the Lady's estate, I began to write to you. A thousand letters, sealed with wax and entrusted to birds that knew the way to your hand. \nAnd each time I pressed the seal, I knew exactly what expression you would wear as you read.\n\nBefore the blood‑stained banquet where red meteors rained through the hall, you liked my letters.\nNot just because I was your favourite swan, but because the Lady was unlike your other kin.\nThough the other swans never spoke of it, I knew their noble patrons mocked you in whispers. That was why we were sent into their houses — to gather their sins behind a veil of dance.\nBut the Lady was not like them. She never uttered a word against you.\nShe said you were admirable — for banishing the brother who once mistreated her.\nShe said you were pitiable — for tossing and turning through the night over your nobles' crimes.\nShe said you were brilliant — for wrapping grand designs in feasts and song.\n\nShe spoke of other things, too: of Yashnia's moon, which she saw each night; of Hyperborea's snow, which she had never seen; of Elysium's flowers, which visited only in dreams.\nBut not once did she ask me to dance. Perhaps she had seen too many swan dances in your palace — or perhaps she had never known a real friend.\nI was disheartened. Swans are born to dance — yet I was left to write, with quills plucked from my own wings.\nAnd still, I hoped — that each time you opened one of those harmless little letters, the furrows on your brow would soften.\nAnd perhaps that is why, on the night of that blood‑drenched banquet, she alone walked out beneath the echo of your thunderous snores.\nI followed her out, as she stepped into the dawn. Her breath misted in the cold, her shoulders trembled — fear of you, or joy for your newfound peace? I could not tell. I only felt relief — that she had never once spoken ill of you. If she had, even had I disobeyed your order, the other swans would have torn her apart like ravens at your command.\nFrom that day on, I was the only swan who never returned to your palace.\nOutwardly, nothing changed: she still shared her stories and wishes for travels with me, and every fourth day I sent you another letter.\nBut perhaps... it only seemed that way. Before the sun rises, all is called night. But the midnight of your thunderous snores, and the final breath before dawn — can they truly be the same?\n\nSince then, I imagine you no longer smile when you read me.\nYou buried yourself in state affairs: reforms, punishments, suspicion. You trusted no one in Yashnia who still breathed. Even birdsong was questioned — for daring to disturb your sleep.\nThe Lady was no exception. Her praise became flattery. Her silence, a hidden slight. Her advice, betrayal.\nHow could you?\n\nAllow me to be plain: from my first autumn in her estate, she let me stop calling myself \"swan.\"\nBy now I expect you have hurled your wineglass in rage, Grand Duke Yushino.\nYou saved me from starvation — made me your ever-dancing swan. But she is the one who taught that swan to be a person.\nShe never asked me to dance — because people are not swans.\nTo protect her, I was ever obedient, ever gentle, ever yours. Always fearing your fury would fall on her.\nBut your suspicion grew like mould in the dark. I defended her, again and again. Until seven days ago — when you summoned her to the palace... and she never returned.\nSo now, I ask just one thing:\n\nIs she still alive?\n\nI no longer wish to be anyone's swan. If she is gone, then I shall tread upon the shards of your shattered glass — even if my feet must bleed to reach you — until your blood spills like wine and dyes your robe crimson.\nBy the time you reach this line, I will already be standing before you, ready to pierce this letter — and your throat — with the very same blade.\"\n\n\"Raise your head and answer me, Yushino!\"\n\n\nParsifal's Note:\nExcept for the final line, the entire scene is sung by Yushino as he reads Momiji's letter aloud. The performance must reflect the full emotional arc within the letter — from deferential restraint, to repressed grief, to seething fury. The true challenge lies in moving seamlessly and convincingly between Yushino's visible reactions and the inner voice of Momiji, gradually rising to its breaking point. It demands exceptional control.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000055"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000056 = {
          ContentEN = "Act Ⅳ – A Speck of Crimson\n\n\"That was only an acci—\"\nThe Lady's death might indeed have been an accident. But even a swan knows: without Grand Duke Yushino's command, no gaoler would dare fetch the tools of torture.\nAnd of all people, the limping figure now standing before him knew Yushino best — and hated him most.\n\nSo before he could finish the sentence, she drove her sword straight through his skull.\n\nThe tip of the blade first reached his tongue — a soft, writhing strip of flesh that split in two, bright red. It reminded her of a serpent's tongue. The Lady had once said she feared nothing more than the eight-headed serpent from Yashnia's ancient myths.\nThen came something firmer — the upper palate. Heavy drinkers often complained of a burning sensation there. Yushino had drunk often, and well. Maybe his was tougher than most. Or maybe not — the steel passed through without resistance.\nAfter that, everything was easy: the hindbrain. Some say it governs sleep. Years ago, Yushino scoured the land for physicians to cure his insomnia. One had told him the problem lay in his hindbrain. She no longer remembered whether that poor man had ended up with his skull smashed or his tongue cut out. Now, Yushino had suffered both. A shattered skull and a severed tongue — quite a poetic ending for him, right?\nBefore his bloodied lips could kiss the edge of her blade, she pulled it free.\nA spray of scarlet spattered a folding screen; the fabric drank it in, the stain blooming like red leaves.\n\nShe remembered the day the Lady told her she no longer needed to call herself a swan.\n\nIt was an autumn afternoon, just before the frost set in. A northern wind from Hyperborea had swept away the last warmth lingering over Yashnia — only to spend all its strength in doing so. The sea turned bitter‑cold overnight. No nobles dared swim; no fishermen dared cast their nets.\nFurther inland, the farmers had just finished harvesting the wheat, yet still fretted that rain might come too soon and spoil the drying grain. Children, sent out reluctantly at the adults' urging, were tasked with picking up the fallen stalks — only to end up bickering over trifles halfway through. From the houses by the fields came the warm scent of fresh bread, baked with the season's first wheat. Cooking smoke curled gently from the chimneys.\nThrough that smoke flew the migratory birds bound south — save one small bird, a ribbon tied to its leg, winging the opposite way. It slipped through the domes and rooftops to deliver the four hundredth secret letter to Grand Duke Yushino.\nIt was his first year on the throne. Amidst sleepless nights, he had only just begun his long campaign — to purge his enemies and secure his rule. Perhaps the gods who govern the seasons took pity on his resolve and granted Yashnia another golden harvest.\nAnd in that hush of ripened wheat, not far from the palace, in a quiet courtyard — a swan said no.\n\nA cold wind stirred. The trees in the Lady's courtyard groaned softly, as if in pain. Red leaves drifted down like blood. One struck the Lady's face. She flinched with a startled yelp — like a cat caught by sudden darkness. The limping one reached out and gently brushed the leaf away.\nThe Lady's cheeks were flushed from the cold. She looked at her and said: \"From now on, you don't have to call yourself a swan. Your name... will be Momiji. Only I will know this. Only I may call you this.\"\n\nThe swan hesitated. She agreed to stop calling herself a bird — but cautiously declined the name Momiji, afraid that if she heard it too often,\nshe might come to love it. And along with it... too many other things.\n\nShe could no longer recall what happened next. Only that, somehow, she had cried — though she never knew quite why.\nTears are warm at first. But they soon turn cold. And so do the red leaves — fiery in hue, cold in the hand.\n\nCold, like Yushino's blood already congealing on marble.\n\nPerhaps it was because she hadn't danced in so long. She might have avoided the spatter, yet one drop landed on the hilt of her sword.\nShe tried to wipe the red away — again and again. But it wouldn't come off. And that brought her back to the moment when a red leaf had fallen onto the Lady's face. She had tried to brush it away, too — but hadn't been able to. As if she were waiting for someone else to do it for her.\nShe lingered again in that autumn, seven years ago — where everything had seemed well.\nSo be it, then. Let that speck of crimson remain.\nShe made no further attempt to wipe the blood away. Nor did she shy from the name Momiji any longer. She turned and walked away — unchallenged, and alone.\n\nThus closed the tale of Momiji: A Chronicle of Rebellion.\n\n\nParsifal's Note:\nThe protagonist's name — Momiji — isn't revealed until the very final act. When the play first debuted, this twist had tremendous impact. But now, after so many stagings, most audiences know it going in. It's a shame — there's nothing quite like the first time.\nHere's an excerpt from the historical record. Might be useful...\n\"According to official chronicles, the third Grand Duke of Yashnia, Yushino, reigned for ten years. In the early years, he purged his political enemies and swept away many of the entrenched ills left by his predecessor, the second Grand Duke. But toward the end of his rule, he grew increasingly suspicious of his own kin. Eventually, he ordered the imprisonment and torture of his sister, resulting in her death. The very next day, he himself was found dead. By then, the fall of Yashnia — to either the Empire or the Elysian Church — had already become inevitable. That the Church ultimately took it... well, that's a matter for another chapter on court conspiracies.\nAs for Yushino's death, three theories persist: suicide brought on by madness, sudden death from chronic alcoholism, and assassination by a swordswoman named Momiji. The third is deemed the least credible — and yet, it remains the version most widely believed. This account is also said to have inspired the now-famous play Momiji: A Chronicle of Rebellion.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000056"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000057 = {
          ContentEN = "Ⅰ. Overture\n\nHast thou ever heard of such a revelry,\n\nWhere profane soldiers and demon's progeny joined hand in hand?\n\nWhere the southerling and the nordling raised a song as one,\n\nTo hymn the silver peace ‘twixt Sola and Charon!\n\nTears of wax did drip from Sola's candlelight,\n\nUpon Charon's face, naught but carefree delight.\n\nThey twisted their waists, they swung limb and limb,\n\nWith steps of dance, their joy did brim.\n\nThrough joy they reached madness, a fevered state,\n\nThrough madness, forgot sorrow and fate.\n\nThrough sorrow, bestowed upon the past its weight,\n\nAnd at the end of past — a name to celebrate!\n\nSoranus Galea!\n\nHe who took an ancient name, yet defied the false god's claim,\n\nHe who inherited an armored surname, to aid the Emperor's frame.\n\nThe younger brother of His Majesty Dis Galea, departed from our world too soon,\n\nSoranus Galea!",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000057"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000058 = {
          ContentEN = "Ⅱ. In Praise of Soranus Galea\n\nNorthward! To lands afar from the false god's gaze,\n\nNorthward! To fields unbound by mortal chains,\n\nNorthward! To realms abundant with liberty!\n\nIn the third early winter since the Exodus,\n\nHouse Galea pressed on, never to repose.\n\nThey swore to flee the Elysian Church's hold,\n\nWith silver blades against the world's end cold.\n\nAt the forefront of that argent tide,\n\nHis form you'd see, striding with pride.\n\nHis helm shone bright, a guiding star afar,\n\nHis steps held firm, like flowers unmarred.\n\nHis eyes gleamed keen, rare diamonds untold,\n\nHis form sped light, as dust on winds old.\n\nSoranus Galea!\n\nTreasure of Dis the First, the household's young wolf,\n\nWhenever he drew his sword, comedy unfolded—\n\nVictory assured, the battle's ending foretold.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000058"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000059 = {
          ContentEN = "Ⅲ. The Gift of Fate\n \n From revelry did tragedy arise;\n \n From the eternal was born what must pass.\n \n South of Lamenting Lake, the snows lay deep.\n \n And those from gentler lands had never beheld such boundless white.\n \n Fallen feathers, ashen earth, lips pale as the ivory lake's milk-stone.\n \n What once was marvel at the snow became dread of death.\n \n Empty bellies growled, heralds of despair unborn.\n \n Even Soranus, fairest and steadfast,\n \n Scoured desperately for a single morsel.\n \n But not even Soranus could conjure wheat from icefields.\n \n He gnawed upon blocks of snow,\n \n Leant upon his longsword as a crutch,\n \n And ventured into the blizzard for one last charge,\n \n Hoping fate concealed a gift beyond the winter's end.\n \n When the storm at last relented,\n \n A village appeared above Lamenting Lake.\n \n With the last of his strength, Soranus knocked upon its gate,\n \n And gave thanks for fate's final, merciful gift.\n \n Yet perhaps, for he and his brother had spurned the gods,\n \n He knew not in that hour the first verse of his downfall was already writ.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000059"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000060 = {
          ContentEN = "Ⅳ. The Snow-Owl's Ballad, Canto Ⅰ\n \n The snow-owl rejoiced, the snow-owl sang,\n \n Thrice it wheeled through the nighted sky,\n \n Seeking fattened mice to still its hunger.\n \n Yet beneath the vault of the Far North,\n \n No breath of life was to be found...\n \n Save for that place — \n \n A hamlet of snow, a village of ice.\n \n The Charon hastened upon the lake's edge ,\n \n While from the south the Solarian sang.\n \n The fish cast off its cloak of brine,\n \n Unfolding blossoms of glistening fat,\n \n More fragrant than the buds of a springvine.\n \n The mash unveiled as its froth fell away,\n \n Revealing crimson draperies of the closing day,\n \n More splendid than autumn's dusk.\n \n Since the day Soranus struck the door of that village by Lamenting Lake,\n \n The feast where strangers became kin has never ceased.\n \n Every Sola laid down their sword,\n \n And embraced the Charon newly met,\n \n Praising their bounty,\n \n Giving thanks for their shelter.\n \n Even Dis Galea, ever wary,\n \n Exchanged oaths with the Charon's chief,\n \n Swearing to treat the Charon as brothers, as dear as Soranus himself,\n \n To stand as one against Filthoids, against winter, against every foe to come.\n \n Even the snow-owl, vigilant hunter, raised its voice in joy,\n \n And sought to alight upon the laden platters that it, too, would share the feast.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000060"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000061 = {
          ContentEN = "Ⅴ. The Snow-Owl's Ballad, Canto Ⅱ\n \n The snow-owl faltered, the snow-owl cried,\n \n Six times it wheeled through the nighted sky.\n \n It longed to feast at the banquet...\n \n Yet as it poised to descend,\n \n A wall of spears and blades arose.\n \n Perhaps the wine had been too sweet,\n \n For the hapless Solarian forgot —\n \n The truth bound in the name \"Charon\": the Children of the Demon.\n \n Children of the Demon, bereft of all virtue, delighting in the defilement of oaths.\n \n Children of the Demon, sly and venomous, weaving their webs of lies.\n \n The salted fish was but prelude to slaughter;\n \n The goblet brimming but crimson-veiled malice.\n \n The Charon chieftain, raising his cup, loosed a baleful laugh —\n \n The kindly feast laid bare as nothing but a snare.\n \n The northern snow fell like Death's scythe;\n \n Could it ever suffer two peoples in peace?\n \n Golden hair dishevelled, silver goblets overturned, iron armour rent.\n \n Swords clamoured loud, driving ever closer upon the Solarian guests.\n \n Arrows sped in volleys, hounding the Solarian like a hunted pack of wolves.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000061"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000062 = {
          ContentEN = "Ⅵ. The Snow-Owl's Ballad, Canto Ⅲ\n \n The snow-owl wept, the snow-owl cried,\n \n Nine times it wheeled through the nighted sky.\n \n It longed to flee the tumult —\n \n Yet cunning are the demons,\n \n Who beguile not only the bewildered bird.\n \n Even Dis Galea, who once struck down a false god,\n \n Deemed himself scorned by Fate,\n \n Doomed to an end unimagined.\n \n Only one carved a path through the tightening snare of the Charon:\n \n Soranus Galea, blazing with wrath at deceit.\n \n In fury he cast a burning candelabrum as a spear, piercing the armour of his foes.\n \n In defiance he swung a brimming silver goblet as a hammer, shattering the helms of the wicked.\n \n Before him, the Charon fell in heaps;\n \n Behind him, stretched a road of blood and flesh.\n \n It was he who had once knocked upon that door,\n \n And so he swore he would depart in whole.\n \n Dis Galea followed close behind,\n \n As though clawing free from a demon's ravenous maw.\n \n He rejoiced that his banner yet stood, unswallowed by the snow.\n \n But Soranus had spent his final strength.\n \n The demons overtook him, their blades came down,\n \n And from that noble brow there burst a white flame.\n \n The glacier crumbled, blood welled forth, hardening into stones of crimson agate.\n \n So fell Soranus Galea, sole brother of Dis Ⅰ.\n \n On the day the Great Migration began, he had sworn to march hand in hand with Death.\n \n Yet none foresaw that Fate would on this day bare its fangs in laughter.\n \n And the snow-owl, too, seeking its feast,\n \n Was struck by an accursed arrow,\n \n Its breath lost amid the bloodied waters.\n \n Alas! Soranus — hero who called for peace...\n \n He never lived to behold the birth of the Hyperborean Empire,\n \n Yet he became its first and truest hero.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000062"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000063 = {
          ContentEN = "Ⅶ. The Proclamation of Vengeance\n \n Dis Galea returned into the snowbound wilderness.\n \n Words forsook him; darkness veiled his sight without end.\n \n In silence he recalled his brother's face.\n \n Had he yet trod the sacred halls of Elysium,\n \n He would have cried aloud the names of the Great Nine,\n \n Entreating the power that guides the fallen home.\n \n But blind faith was long renounced, the idols cast down,\n \n And death itself bore witness: the gods were gone.\n \n So the host kept silence, and with Dis Galea they mourned wordlessly.\n \n The soldiers, wrath bound in silence, awaited his command.\n \n The frozen earth trembled beneath his wrath.\n \n Three days and three nights his silence endured.\n \n The host waited, and waited still — for their lord to call forth vengeance, to cast the demons into dust.\n \n At last he spoke, slow and measured: \"Leave these snows. Let us bide our time.\n \n The Charon are indeed our foes; yet more loathsome still is our own frailty.\n \n The demons bear no Solarian virtue. They wield their strength in wanton excess.\n \n Then must we forge a strength redoubled, to cast them down and bind them fast in chains.\n \n Better to gather strength, to lie low in wait for a just vengeance,\n \n Than to squander rage to our common ruin.\"",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000063"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000064 = {
          ContentEN = "Ⅷ. The Iron Hooves in Their Charge\n \n The host stirred and swelled like waters brought to the boil.\n \n Far-sighted was Dis Galea,\n \n Prudent was Dis Galea!\n \n And so they mourned with Dis Galea for Solanus,\n \n For Solanus, revered and feared, had fallen.\n \n Dirges rose and tears fell,\n \n As though a thousand stars descended all at once.\n \n The night lay veiled in mist; no guiding star remained.\n \n This day we endure the bitterness of deceit;\n \n Yet when the tide of the Great Migration next sweeps the snowbound plain:\n \n Though death descends as equal fate on every soul, Sola and Charon alike,\n \n The demons' blood shall drench the earth.\n \n Their bones shall be wrought into goblets;\n \n Their houses shall become graveyards;\n \n Their horns shall be broken and cast into Lamenting Lake, until its waters brim with ruin.\n \n And this land, bright with Phoxene, shall rise as offering unto Soranus.\n \n Thus, feeding on hatred,\n \n Dis Galea and his host endured the winter.\n \n They unsheathed their swords and seized the hamlets;\n \n They tightened their reins and trampled the houses.\n \n Yet endless massacre slowly dulled their wrath,\n \n And the Solarian, guided by reason, turned from the path of carnage and blood, \n \n And sought instead a road of concord.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000064"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000065 = {
          ContentEN = "Ⅸ. The Dissolving of Hatred\n \n Ten times did the ice of Lamenting Lake melt, and ten times it froze anew.\n \n And in the villages the Charon's dread deepened day by day —\n \n For ten years past it was they who had plotted a massacre that failed,\n \n And it was they who must now taste its bitter fruit.\n \n Dis Galea, clinging to life by Fate's frail thread,\n \n Endured the harsh winters of the North by means unknown,\n \n And with the silver tide of his host he laid waste to every land round about.\n \n At last he came,\n \n And with his coming an ending surely fell.\n \n With his host he cast down in swift might the fortress reared upon Soranus's grave.\n \n The Charon, in despair, murmured prayers to Mother Embla,\n \n Believing that this day would see them sink into the Misty Sea's embrace.\n \n Yet what came was neither slaughter nor conquest,\n \n But leniency beyond all measure.\n \n Dis Galea cursed Fate,\n \n Yet ten years of war had bent his heart toward peace.\n \n In his letters he wrote thus, grave and rational:\n \n \"Vengeance is scarlet, fair to behold — yet it only ends in withering.\n \n Peace is silver, grievous to bear — yet it composes the song of prosperity.\n \n Even if we let the Charon's blood sink into the stone, yet the stone shall one day crumble to desert.\n \n But once the seeds of peace are sown, for centuries, for millennia, flowers shall bloom.\"\n \n \"My Solarian brothers, burdened with hatred — let us together cast scorn upon Fate.\n \n To deny anger its dominion over the mind — that is the very proof of our humanity.\n \n These Charon, once branded Children of the Demon, \n \n Are righteous folk who rose against the Church.\n \n Rather than cross swords and raise the dirge,\n \n Let Soranus be the first and last sacrifice.\n \n And the Charon shall mourn him with us as sisters and brothers.\n \n So long as Lamenting Lake endures,\n \n So long as the mountains stand,\n \n The name of Soranus shall be sung without end, echoing between mountain and lake.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000065"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000066 = {
          ContentEN = "Ⅹ. Silver Candles Aflame, the Carnival Begins\n \n Thus did Galea's soldiers, the Solarian host who had reached the uttermost North,\n \n Lay down their swords, set aside their hatred,\n \n And lifted silver candles on high with hearts of mercy.\n \n Following close upon Galea, they came unto the dwellings of the Charon.\n \n White mountains stretched, the keen winds howled,\n \n And the Charon, who thought the flame of war was at hand, beheld instead from afar the silver candlelight.\n \n They marvelled: the Solarian host comes not for vengeance, but for peace!\n \n Dis I had lost a brother;\n \n Yet were not the Charon themselves a vaster host of brethren?\n \n Then the Charon brought forth their finest wine,\n \n With meats newly cured and berries preserved.\n \n They pledged kinship with Dis I,\n \n And hailed the gracious, gentle Galea as their lord.\n \n They raised a city meant to endure — \n \n Walls, a great plaza, lofty halls, and a theatre of song.\n \n There, Solarian and Charon alike\n \n Would share their finest days without divide.\n \n All who heard Dis I proclaim that vision were enthralled;\n \n And each vowed to be the cornerstone of the Empire's enduring dream.\n \n From that day, with each returning year, \n \n In Icelake — where Soranus fell and the tragedy found its end — \n \n The people gather.\n \n They kindle candles in silver candlesticks,\n \n And strike the festival's first note in the Far North.\n \n The flames soar, recalling ages past;\n \n The bells resound long, and grief is cast away.\n \n So hear — hear the songs that shall never fade;\n \n Sing — sing the hymn to the everlasting Empire.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000066"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000067 = {
          ContentEN = "Charon brothers, Charon sisters — remember that verse they drilled into us when we were kids?\n\"Have you heard of such a carnival, where blasphemers clasp the hands of Demon's Children?\"\nYeah, that one. The Silvercandle Hymn. We've heard it so many times our ears grew numb.\nEvery year, when that day comes, we Charon — scraping through bitter lives — stitch together masks and costumes. Because only then do the Solarian pretend to treat us as human. Only then do they hold back their scorn. They dance, they laugh — and they blast that cursed Hymn at us a hundred times over.\nAnd I know many of you can recite its lines, maybe even feel sorry for the \"kind-hearted\" Dis I and his brother Soranus. Some even believe — because our ancestors supposedly killed Soranus — that we're bound to serve the Solarian forever. They say it's our destiny. To repay a blood debt. I say that's all lies.\nDon't buy it.\nDon't buy it!\nDon't buy it!!!\nThe Silvercandle Carnival is a lie from start to finish. There never was a carnival. Only a massacre. And Soranus? He wasn't some poor victim. He was the butcher — the worst of them all!\n \nListen, I'll tell you another story. The truth.\n \nOur ancestors, the Charon, lived in the North. Because of Deterioration, they didn't build great cities — just small scattered villages. One clan lived by Lamenting Lake, warming themselves with Phoxene from the Heaventree nearby, living on what little they had.\nThen came rumours from the South — trouble in the Elysian Church, rebels driven out, exiles wandering north. At first it was just gossip. Elysium was too far to matter. Until Solarian soldiers showed up, armed, on our lake shore.\nOur ancestors pitied them. Thought they'd never survive the northern winter. So they took them in, gave them food.\nThat winter was cruel, as if Father Ask and Mother Embla cursed us with the storm. But the cold didn't kill them — it only fed their greed. Galea, their leader, seemed harmless enough. But his brother? A greedy wolf. The first time he stepped into the village, his eyes went straight to our granaries. Already he was thinking of plunder.\nAnd on the night meant for peace, that brute Soranus had his blades and shields ready. At the feast our ancestors prepared, he struck.\nBy dawn, except for a handful of hunters out in the wilds, none were left alive... Galea built Icelake on our ancestors' graves, and from it rose his Empire. His men enslaved us, lied to us, branded us sinners.\n \nThink about it! Really think about it! The Hymn — every line is about the Solarian. Not once does it name a single Charon. Why? Because they didn't know? Or because they never cared?\nThe Silvercandle Carnival? A sham. The Hymn? Lies. They boast, \"On this day we're equal; on this day we dance together.\" Liars! That wall between Solarian and Charon? They built it! And Soranus? He wasn't a hero or martyr. He was a filthy butcher!\nDon't be fooled. Boycott the Silvercandle Carnival!",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000067"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000068 = {
          ContentEN = "Welcome to The Asphodel. Our tavern serves drinks from every corner of the continent. And don't worry — we always collect the coin before you pass out.\n \n The Hyperborean Empire:\n Most of our Empire's brews find their way into the bellies of alchemists and soldiers, so the names may not sound drinkable... yet the taste is finer than you'd think. Craving something strong? Just say the word!\n Legion Wolfblood\n Standard issue across the Legions — a spirit strong enough to burn off fear on the battlefield and double as a battlefield disinfectant. The taste is strong — deliberately so, they say, to stop soldiers from overindulging.\n \n Phoxichor Engine Lubricant\n A favourite among Imperial alchemists. Named for its uncanny resemblance — in both colour and texture — to the actual lubricant used in Phoxichor engines. Rumour has it one absent-minded alchemist once took a swig of the real thing by mistake.\n \n Fortress Barrage\n A fiery spirit from the Empire's southern reaches. Best downed in one go to savour the full effect — like a fortress cannon blasting through your body, leaving your limbs no longer your own. Intense. Devastating. Glorious.\n \n Laurelled Delight\n The go-to toast when a Charon earns their Laurel. Often shaken into a froth for the ceremony — but beneath the bubbles lies a crisp, refreshing flavour, delightful on the tongue.\n \n Huaxu:\n If spirits are too strong for your taste, why not try something from the Eastern realm? Unlike the grape wines we know, theirs are brewed from rice — smooth, mellow, and golden as amber. And with Huaxu's travelling merchants scattered far and wide, these delicacies are never far from hand. In the mood for something different? Just say the word!\n Tiger's Soul\n An amber-hued rice wine with a rich, mellow depth — said to carry the essence of a tiger's spirit. In Huaxu, it is a common daily draught, a medicinal tonic, and a warming comfort all in one.\n \n Peach Blush\n A light, sweet wine with the fragrance of fresh fruit. Blended with peach gum, its texture is as soft to chew as it is to sip. Its delicate blush has long made it a favourite among ladies.\n \n A Goblet Above\n A Huaxu proverb says: \"One day above is a year below.\" True to its name, a single goblet of this potent brew sends you into a deep slumber — and when you wake, much time will have fled. Best not overindulge — lest you find yourself in Heaven sooner than you wished.\n \n Azure Dragon's Draught\n In Huaxu, the Charon are honoured as Loongscions — descendants of the Eastern dragon. This is their drink of choice: crisp as a mountain spring, with a finish that lingers like the breath of a Loong.\n \n Republic of Luca:\n You may know Luca for its artists, sailors, dreamers and madcaps... but did you know it also gave birth to a world of cocktails? Try them, and you'll be won over by their rich layers and flights of fancy. They rarely leave one truly drunk... but they do have a way of stirring inspiration. So, what shall it be? Just say the word!\n Palette\n A cascade of spirits, layered in dazzling colours — a painting in a glass. Stir it, and all brilliance collapses into black.\n \n Masquerade Rhapsody\n Not one drink, but ten — each wearing the same disguise. You won't know what lies within until you taste it... just as you never truly know the face behind a mask at the ball.\n \n Poet's Rhyme\n Soft upon the tongue, yet echoing long after — like the rhyme that closes a verse. Poets and playwrights drink deep in pursuit of inspiration... sometimes yielding a masterpiece, and sometimes, a grand puddle of vomit.\n \n Chisel\n A rare Luca spirit, named by sailors who once gazed upon statues crated in their holds and thought of the tool that shaped them. True to its name, it etches fire through the body with each swallow.\n \n Aethyrie:\n Do scholars drink? Indeed they do. They drink to dull the sting of failure... and to raise a jubilant toast with a ringing \"Eureka!\" when discovery strikes. Like knowledge, wine intoxicates all the same. The only pity is that even in naming it, they cannot resist teaching a lesson. If such hidden meanings amuse you — just say the word.\n Truth in the Cup\n Aethyrie's scholars once argued fiercely over the world's first principle — some claimed it was air, others fire, others still countless tiny particles. At last, they agreed on one thing: whatever its origin, it must be plentiful in wine, for nothing else so swiftly brings joy, and peace among rivals.\n \n Philosopher's Tears\n One tale tells of a philosopher who, upon realising that the diagonal of a square vessel could not be expressed by rational number, wept until his tears ran dry, and bled instead. Thus, the crimson hue of this wine. Aethyrie's scholars adore the tale. Of course, the truth is simpler and far less poignant: it's just the colour of grapes.\n \n O, Bounty!\n Brewed only in years of true abundance, this wine heralds the coming of plenty. Once the vats are filled, the people of Aethyrie gather in celebration — drinking deep, lifting their voices in song or spirited debate, rejoicing in the bounty of the year.\n \n The Syllogism\n Major premise: wine brings inspiration.\n Minor premise: inspiration is the wellspring of creation.\n Conclusion: wine is the wellspring of creation.\n In Aethyrie, only students sharp enough to name the fallacy earn the right to drink it. Luckily for you, here in Hyperborea, no riddle need be solved before your glass is filled.\n \n Khemet:\n Life in the desert is harsh, its people rugged — yet their welcome is as warm as the sands are fierce. When Starweavers or Phoxhunters pass through their villages, the locals pour out drinks as bold and mysterious as the desert itself. This tavern may not lie among the dunes, but our welcome is no less hearty. Care to taste the desert wind? Just say the word!\n Black Honey\n Dark as pitch, yet heavy with sweetness. Its deep shade comes from carbon — said to draw out every impurity.\n \n Serpent's Kiss\n Rumoured to be laced with snake venom, it numbs the tongue with a venomous tingle and chills the body with a creeping cool. In truth, it's nothing but fizzing gases.\n \n Experimental Dregs\n A dangerous concoction brewed from industrial alcohol and leftover dregs of countless alchemical experiments. They say you might find a lizard's claw or rat's tail adrift within. Only the daring drink it — more a test of courage than a toast.\n \n The Burning Wind\n Steeped with spice and peppers, this fiery brew scours the throat like the desert wind itself. One mouthful, and you're carried to Khemet's sands in all their scorching glory.\n \n The Elysian Church:\n To the south lies the Elysian Church — the Empire's sworn foe. Its folk drown in scripture, bowing to false gods in blind faith... hopeless, if you ask me. But their wines — now those are worth a taste. Rare, costly, and hard to come by. If your purse is heavy enough, just say the word!\n The Tenth God\n The war with Elysium grinds on, and so their wares are forbidden in the Empire. Even so, one brew still finds its way to our shelves: The Tenth God. It's named in jest — as if one drink could rival the blessings of all nine gods. Perhaps that sly irreverence is why the officials turned a blind eye — a blasphemous name, for a drink too fine to ban.\n \n Traditional Charon Brews:\n The mountains beyond the Empire's northern edge are not empty. There, the Charon who still defy its yoke dwell — honouring their Father Ask and Mother Embla. They brew their own drinks for rites that preserve their faith and legend. Sometimes, these brews are traded for a handful of coins. They're not strong, but in Icelake's biting cold, nothing warms you better. If you'd warm your bones — and hear a tale of the Charon — just say the word!\n Heaventree Sap\n A light, clear blue liquor that recalls the gleam of Phoxene. Unlike Phoxichor Engine Lubricant, it runs bright and fluid. Its name honours the Heaventrees — sacred to Charon tradition.\n \n Borealberry Brew\n Brewed from Borealberries — a prized fruit of Icelake and the far north — this wine is fragrant and best enjoyed steaming hot. The Charon may not fear the cold, but none would turn down a sweet, steaming glass of fruit-rich delight in the heart of winter.\n \n \n We don't serve minors. And don't bother with \"it's for my parents\" — that tale's worn thin.\n Also, no vomiting in the tavern. You'll pay for the cleaning... and we'll throw in a good thrashing, free of charge.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000068"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000069 = {
          ContentEN = "The \"Benefactor\" — so named for their role as giver — is one of the few Forsaken leaders whose existence can be confirmed. True to their name, they are famed for immense wealth. Before we go further, two points must be made clear: first, the Forsakens are not a profit-driven organisation; second, most of their members possess no honest livelihood.\n \nIndeed, the Forsakens care little for profit — their focus lies squarely in undermining the order of the Hyperborean Empire. Other resistance groups may raid treasuries or stage kidnappings for ransom, but the Forsakens have never stooped to such schemes. This makes them easy to distinguish: if a crime was committed for money, it likely wasn't their doing. The Forsakens care only for destruction. Alchemy factories, interregional roads, and the manors of prominent officials are among their favourite targets. Some claim this pattern reflects a kind of moral fastidiousness in their founders. I cannot agree. Morality restrains those who revere rules — but given the Forsakens' origins and conduct, one can hardly argue that morality plays any meaningful part in shaping them.\n \nAccording to statistics gathered by Warden Hellfire, at least half the Forsaken members now imprisoned had already been convicted of other crimes before joining the organisation. Thugs, petty thieves, armed robbers — criminals unmoved by the law, let alone by morality. Yet once inside the Forsakens, none of them committed crimes for money alone. This can only mean the organisation upholds a code more binding than law — and possesses financial resources sufficient to sustain its members' lives, even to fuel their crimes. From this, Warden Hellfire drew a prediction: among the Forsakens' leaders, there must be one who bears the cost of the organisation's daily needs. As the Forsakens grew active again and more members were thrown behind bars, a certain codename began surfacing in warden reports — \"Benefactor.\"\n \nThe \"Benefactor\" is said to reach out in one's hour of deepest desperation, sending a letter with whatever the recipient most craves. Most often it is money; at times, something bloodier — a grisly token of vengeance, such as the severed limb of a sworn enemy. Though recipients may technically refuse these sudden acts of generosity, the timing is so uncannily precise that in practice, no one ever does. And so, wastrels and dangerous marauders alike find themselves drawn into the Forsakens. When their old vices drag them back into hardship, another letter arrives. After several such cycles, they become puppets — marionettes strung with coin, dancing at the Benefactor's whim. The tasks assigned differ little from their former crimes. A thief is told to break the chains on an alchemy factory gate. A bomber, to plant charges beside its Phoxichor engine. A repeat escapee, to present himself before the constables — drawing their attention elsewhere. Thus, strangers who've never met, acting solely on the direction of letters from a single hand, carry out crimes near impossible to unravel. None of them knows whether they have accomplices, whether they're the bait in someone else's scheme, or what the mission's true purpose might be. Such is the Benefactor's method. Though their schemes leave ample traces, the Benefactor makes no real effort to conceal their presence. To the wardens, each letter sent to an underling reads like an open declaration of war. And so, in the years when the Benefactor was most active, wardens across the Hyperborean Empire poured untold hours into unmasking them.\n \nIn theory, it should have been simple. The Benefactor's letters came with great sums of money — and wealth, after all, leaves a trail. Soon enough, the wardens caught a supposed Benefactor: a cloth merchant in Scarletriver who had recently struck it rich. He claimed he had taken up the name out of outrage at the Empire's oppression of the Charon, and that his crimes were acts of rebellion. To any clear-eyed observer, it was a flimsy excuse. No ordinary merchant could bankroll a resistance force spanning the continent. This \"Benefactor\" was nothing but a decoy — a scapegoat. So the wardens pressed on. One captured, then another... more than ten in all, over the span of two years. Yet the Forsakens' resistance showed no sign of waning. There could be no doubt: above these pretenders stood a true magnate — one wealthy enough to bankroll a dozen prosperous merchants at once.\n \nSo the wardens followed every lead, tracing financial channels in search of the true Benefactor. But they found nothing — no records of large transfers, no sign of hidden accounts. The merchants they'd seized came from every walk of life: different trades, origins, ages, genders, even quirks of personality. Their only common ground was this: each had found sudden success in recent years, and each commanded considerable wealth. Every one of them swore the funds used to support Forsaken operations came from their own trade — and all, with striking unanimity, claimed they had adopted the name \"Benefactor\" out of discontent with the Empire's oppression of the Charon. It was all too familiar. Just as Forsaken criminals ignored the law but never broke their own code, so too must these merchants have been lured by profit — or threatened into compliance — to act in such perfect unison.\n \nThe wardens reached a chilling conclusion: however the true Benefactor passed their money to these seemingly law-abiding merchants, they must possess insight beyond ordinary men — and a hand ruthless enough to enforce obedience. How many such merchants might already dance to their tune was impossible to say. If the merchants were fishermen chasing the tides along the shore, then the Benefactor was the one who commanded the sea itself. A trace of fear crept into the wardens' hearts. Perhaps this Benefactor truly stood at some higher vantage — and perhaps their very investigation was already unfolding in the palm of their hand.\n \nYet for most ordinary folk, the details scarcely matter. They know only this: that within the Forsakens is a leader known as the Benefactor — one of Atlasia's three great magnates, spoken of in the same breath as Ms Zhiliu of Huaxu and Lord Generous, one of the Seven Virtues of Elysium. Tavern chatter delights in speculation: how many zeroes trail the sum of their fortune; how many roast turkeys and lambs vanish at their table in a single feast; whether the candles in their manor ever need be blown out to save on lamp oil... And for those at the end of their rope, one last hope remains: that when they wake from their dreams, a letter from the Benefactor will be waiting before them.\n ",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000069"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000070 = {
          ContentEN = "Sacred Tower Era, Year 1–500: The Filthoid Waves\n \nLong millennia past, the world was believed to consist of but two realms: in the south, the theocracy of Elysium; in the north, only untamed wilds. Elysium was a land consecrated to the Great Nine, where each community served its chosen deity. To the Patriarch the gods gave revelation, which was passed down through the ranks of the priesthood. These revelations, however, spoke only of reverence, awe, and piety — with little bearing on life, law, or anything of practical worth. In Elysium, life changed little from day to day. The Sacred Canon dictated when to rise, when to labour, when to take meals, and when to kneel in prayer and confession at the halls of praise. The faithful believed that when their time came, they would ascend to the Heaven above Heavens — a realm where honey ran in rivers and even the air itself was sweet. To us it may seem a stagnant existence — yet to those of that age, it was both the best and only way to live. To remain within the Church's fold was life; to leave it was to embrace near-certain death.\n \nAt that time, Filthoids were the shared plight of all humankind.\n \nThese were no ordinary beasts, but creatures that came in hordes — possessed of strength and cunning beyond common animals, and a boundless hatred for mankind. Be they Solarian, countless though they were, or the horned but fewer-numbered Charon, none could stand alone against a Filthoid pack. In the south, the Great Nine bestowed their might upon the faithful of Elysium. Their divine power was forged into sacred relics, and whoso bore them served as a vessel of the gods' authority, spreading their grace across the land. Only the most virtuous and pure of heart were chosen to wield such relics. These champions gathered followers and smote the Filthoids, their deeds honoured with titles echoing the Seven Virtues.\n \nYet as the wars wore on, some of these paragons fell in battle — and amid the chaos of the front lines, there was neither time to return their relics to the Holy Capital, nor to perform the long rites of selection and investiture. So with centuries passing thus, the relics ceased to return to the Holy Capital, and were passed instead from bearer to bearer, guided by the will of the predecessors. These heirs became the first knight-commanders. Around them gathered retainers and the faithful alike, forming the first knightly orders.\n \nIn their wars against the Filthoids, each knightly order honed a style of war all their own. Commander Patience favoured the hammer-and-anvil. Clad in full plate, his heavy infantry would draw the Filthoid swarms upon themselves. While the beasts clawed in vain at walls of iron, the Commander led his cavalry forth from ambush — crashing in from all sides like a god's own hammer, to crush the foe between them. By contrast, the order of Generosity practised war with splendour. Their commander, an archer without peer, commanded the largest host of bowmen in Elysium. Before battle, he would seize a high ridge for his killing ground, arraying his archers in scattered bands to cover every angle. Their storm of arrows would break the Filthoids before they ever reached the lines — so that in the best of cases, the heavy infantry might win the day without spilling a drop of blood.\n \nThrough long years of war these tactics reached refinement, and the southern reaches of the continent were slowly drawn beneath the Church's dominion. Yet the shadow of failure never strayed far. In the five-hundredth year of the Sacred Tower Era, a horde rose greater than any before. As though guided by cunning, the Filthoids bypassed several knightly garrisons and drove straight for the Church's heart — the Holy Capital itself. By the time the orders grasped the threat, no hour remained to marshal stratagems or shape the field. They could only stand with mortal flesh against a countless swarm, fighting in chaos and desperation. Mankind's one advantage over the Filthoids lay in the discipline of ordered war. Once battle collapsed into primal struggle, the outcome could be nothing but tragedy. Midway through the clash, the orders of Loyalty and Humility were slain to the last — commanders and cohorts alike. Even the order of Diligence, famed for the might of its individual warriors, fared little better: their commander lost an arm to the beasts' onslaught. Just as the remaining knights steeled themselves to perish, resolved that no Filthoid would breach the high walls of the Holy Capital, a miracle was wrought. From the central spire of the city rose a perfect moon, which descended to the heart of the battlefield. Thus appeared the ninth of the Great Nine — the first to reveal divinity — the Goddess of the Full Moon and Fate. The Sacred Canon of Elysium speaks of many wonders, oft dismissed as embellishment. Yet the chronicles of this descent record marvels more astounding still — eclipsing even the Canon's own miracles.\n \nThe goddess raised her sacred right arm and pointed to the heavens. The sky split asunder, revealing its black foundation. With her finger she struck that dark vault ten times, and at each touch a light was kindled. At first no larger than a pinhole, the lights swelled — to the size of camels, then of hippos — until at last they plunged earthward. The roars of the falling lights surpassed the bellow of a hundred lions. Their blasts scorched like molten iron, driving before them clouds of poisoned dust. The Filthoids, driven to frenzy by the goddess's falling lights, burst apart — their forms shattered, their essence undone. Yet the faithful who fought in her name were bathed in that same radiance and felt not fire, but coolness. Though their eyes beheld a vision of hellfire, their skin knew only the touch of spring water — and in their hearts welled a rapture beyond measure. Such was the goddess's grace.\n \nThus was the second great crisis of the Elysian Church ended under the goddess's protection. In later ages, some would claim the chronicles of this miracle were pure invention. Yet the knights who endured the battle became zealots unshakable — vowing to spend their every drop of sweat and blood in defence of the Holy Capital. Fortunate indeed that never again did a Filthoid wave of such scale rise upon Elysian soil.\n \nYet across the continent lay lands untouched by divine gaze. At the Church's northernmost bounds stretched the Emerald Forest. Beyond it lay country unknown: not barren, but rich with plains and ample rain. But long harried by Filthoids, men knew well what awaited any who strayed beyond the gods' protection and the knights' guard: it was no different from exile into a perilous wilderness. A few fervent preachers, undaunted by death, ventured northward — but seldom returned whole. To the common folk of Elysium, daily life may have mirrored the pages of the Canon a little too closely, yet even tedium was preferable to death. Thus it was only dimly known that a vast land stretched beyond the north, equal in breadth to the Church's own dominion. Few gave thought to that Filthoid-haunted land — not until the Great Migration of Sacred Tower Year 998.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000070"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000071 = {
          ContentEN = "Sacred Tower Era, Year 900–998: The Eve of Uprising\n \nIn Sacred Tower Year 998, a great uprising swept across the southern continent, shaking even the seemingly unshakable Elysian Church. Though the revolt failed to overthrow the Church, its remnants fled north — founding a succession of new realms. Together, these events became known as the Great Migration.\n \nFor a thousand years, the Church upheld its theocracy. From high priest to commoner, all believed this order eternal — shielding the world from the Filthoid threat beyond, and sustaining life within, day after day, until the end of time. Yet each new Filthoid wave, though deepening fear and strengthening the Church's authority, quietly set in motion changes that reshaped the land itself.\n \nThe Filthoid invasions placed different burdens upon each stratum of Elysian society.\n \nForemost among the burdened were the knightly orders, who suffered grievous losses in every Filthoid invasion. Yet after each war, they rebounded with startling speed. The valour of the knights — and the claim that they had seen gods with their own eyes — inspired the common folk. Thus the faithful flocked to join the orders. Most would never rise to knighthood, serving instead as common soldiers, yet they felt thereby closer to the divine, and some even yielded their lands willingly to the orders.\n \nThus the knightly orders — once purely martial — became institutions of military, economic, commercial, and even religious power. Their devotion to the Great Nine merged with personal loyalty to their commanders. From this fusion of faith and wealth emerged a new social force: the military nobility.\n \nThey were called nobility because the post of knight-commander — once granted by divine choice, election, or contest — began to pass by blood. Commanders' kin filled the orders' ranks, managing finances, land disputes, and rites. In the more arrogant orders, it's said their feasts even outshone ceremonies for the gods. At the time few thought this amiss — but later events proved the danger. For when reverence for a man outstrips reverence for the gods, that man holds power enough to cast them down.\n \nNext came the plight of the freeholding farmers. Before the Filthoid invasions, most lived near towns in modest self-sufficiency. But war left many lands desolate. Those who lost their fields sought the protection of knight-commanders — becoming tenant farmers, bound to their estates. At the same time, rebuilding the ravaged regions gave rise to a new force: the merchant.\n \nThese merchants were no peddlers or wandering hawkers. They bought up the goods of entire regions, then sold them wholesale to lands under reconstruction. Their trade spanned vast distances — yet many never left their grand estates. All was moved by money alone, while the dispossessed farmers became mere cogs within their elaborate designs.\n \nAt first, these merchants still feared the Church's edicts, taking only what they deemed a rightful share. But in time, their sense of what was \"rightful\" grew warped — and their fortunes soared to staggering heights.\n \nAs these great magnates rose, the farmers' plight grew ever more dire. When a farmer stood before not just a merchant, but a knight-commander too, he bore a double yoke — of coin and of arms. In famine years — or even sooner — he might sell his land, fall into tenancy, and still be forced to offer tribute for knightly rites. Such lives were no longer bearable. Yet many clung to the belief that all was divine will... until a new thought stirred: Why would the gods ordain such misery for us?\n \nAnd what of the gods' own servants? The clergy — those closest to divinity — remained secure in their sanctuaries, faithfully obeying oracles from the Holy Capital. In principle, they stood untouched by the turmoil — and in truth, most were. Yet some began to think otherwise. A few even joined the uprising and the Great Migration. Their numbers in the resistance were few — but their influence, profound.\n \nAt the root lay one question: What is divinity?\nIs it a perfect being, who split its holiness into nine — becoming the Great Nine?\nOr are the Nine each perfect — distinct, yet united in will, together forming the foundation of creation?\nWere the Nine born in sequence? And if so, is one greater than the rest?\nAbove the gods... is there yet another god?\n \nFor centuries, such questions plagued Elysium's theologians. At last, in Sacred Tower Year 480, during the Eighth Universal Council, they reached a fragile consensus:\nDivinity is manifold — its consciousness encompassing all things. It divided itself into nine, and so came the Great Nine: equal and one.\nThe divine is perfect, whole. All that occurs is its flawless design — both change and changelessness. For the divine is the immutable beyond change, the necessary beyond chance.\nThe divine is infinite, abiding at the beginning and end of time — the primal cause of all things.\n \nThe clergy debated for more than a century, forging a fragile accord. Yet just twenty years later, in Year 500, the Filthoid Waves shattered it.\n \nThe descent of the Goddess of the Full Moon and Fate proved divinity was real — yet also sparked new doubts: Were the Nine truly equal?\nIf all things are ordained by the divine... then were the Filthoid Waves too? And if so — why did the divine descend in person to end them?\n \nThose already discontent with the Universal Councils' decrees found these questions echoing within. Their doubts mirrored the farmers': Could the gods truly be trusted?\nAs faith gave way to scepticism, some priests turned to old records — devising tests and studies to prove whether the divine existed at all.\n \nThe eight Universal Councils, once meant as pillars of doctrine, became weapons of dissent. The First was overturned by the Seventh; the Sixth absolved those condemned by the Second. Line after line of contradiction chipped away at the very idea of divine truth.\n \nIn response, the Church created a new role — the scholastic priests — tasked with quelling doubt and realigning thought. Over centuries, their efforts slowly brought unity back to the clergy. Yet a few remained fixated on old texts and speculative works, preferring ancient scrolls over new teachings. They were few, and the Church dismissed them as harmless cranks, lost in their books.\n \nAmong those few scholars stood a curious young priest named Alexis. He dreamed of writing a tome that answered every question — a book to mend the flaws in doctrine. To do so, he left the Holy Capital, journeying through the dioceses to see how the gods' grace truly moved among the people. He recorded all he saw.\n \nBut Alexis never returned. In time, he gained another name — the Sage. He joined with Dis Galea of the Three-Wolf Order, and together they led the uprising and opened a new chapter of history.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000071"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000072 = {
          ContentEN = "\"Truth is, my whole world's no bigger than the patch of dirt in front of me. All that grand talk the gentry fuss over — who's right, who's wrong, what Lord Argent is up to — I can't make heads or tails of it. The High Chancellor of Loongyuan, the Outer Peaks folk, the Jishu Sect, the Xuanheng Sect... whoever they are, not one of them's ever made my crops grow an inch taller.\nSo before the Scourge of Zhuyin came, I'd never once imagined that the skinny lad we all called Scrap could be any kind of Drifter.\nWhen the fields were quiet, I'd hear Old Wang ramble on about the people from the Outer Peaks — men with swords and wine gourds, hopping over rooftops like it was nothing, coming and going without a trace, living free as birds. Even if they didn't know any secret arts, they still looked the part: straight-backed, well turned out, just like the masters in the old stories.\nBut Scrap? All year round he's in the same torn vest, ragged shorts, and a beaten-up straw hat. From far off he looks more like a monkey than a boy. Come summer he's baked so dark he turns red. Not a single thing about him said \"master\" of any kind.\nWho'd have thought that when those Sable Bi'an came tearing in, it'd be that scrawny scrap of a boy who'd end up saving our skins?\nThose Bi'an came fast — too fast for us to burn a stick of incense or run begging to the gentry for help. People died; fields were ripped apart. Just when we thought it was the end for us, Scrap stepped forward with his hoe. Told me to get the others out. Said he was a Drifter from the Outer Peaks, and that he had to stand his ground and hold those beasts back.\nI was scared half to death, so I did what he told me. But on the way down the path, I couldn't help looking back. Think of it — it might've been the only time in my life I'd ever see someone like the heroes in those old tales.\nBut what I saw wasn't some great hero in fine robes.\nIt was just a lad like us... Then a Bi'an knocked him flying — blood everywhere. Yet he dragged himself up by that hoe and hurled himself at them again. And again. Until every last one of us had run far enough that they couldn't see us anymore...\nThat's how it happened. So... do you know how to get word to the Outer Peaks? I want to take Scrap's bones back there, along with the letter he left behind. He's the one who saved my life. And he's the one who taught me this: a hero isn't made by the skills he's got — it's the brave, honest heart that counts...\"",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000072"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000073 = {
          ContentEN = "In Huaxu, one ought not rely on brute force — yet, as we all know, words can cut just as sharply.\nThus did I spend years roaming the land, observing every quarrel, retort, and petty squabble I could find, and distilled the entire art of human rhetoric into four supreme manoeuvres: LMAO, copypasta, bootlicking, and seething. Master these, and you shall never again lose ground in an argument.\nAllow me to present a few examples. First, the proper deployment of LMAO...\n \n(The rest of the pages have been brutally crossed out. You close the book, and on the back cover you find a single bold word scrawled across it: copypasta.)",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000073"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000074 = {
          ContentEN = "Jishu's great — oh, truly great;\nJishu's bright — oh, really bright!\nOur wooden steed — yes, made of wood — runs a thousand miles outright!\nCogs spin nonstop — whirr, whirr, whirr — even Lord Argent gawks in awe!\nJishu's good — so good, so fine;\nclever, crafty — top of the line!\nCome join us now — come one, come all;\nlife turns easy — easy and light!\nFree and breezy — ee-ee-ee, yeah-yeah-yeah!\n \n— Gongshang Shuo",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000074"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000075 = {
          ContentEN = "Chapter Five — \"The New High Chancellor\"\n\n\"Useless wretch!\"\nThe slap cracked through the hall like a whip. The boy reeled, clutching his cheek, his gaze darting sideways — and that timid look only made the old man before him tremble with even sharper fury.\n\"Our family has served in high office within the Xuanheng Sect for generations! And yet it produced you — a useless, good-for-nothing failure! After all the toil and sweat I spent raising you, is this what I'm repaid with? How are you meant to face our honoured forebears!?\"\n\"Alright, Zhao — breathe.\"\nA soft, feminine voice drifted from a bench in the corner. Its owner, robed in green, narrow-featured, a glimmer coiled deep behind the eyes, looked almost like a green serpent draped in human skin.\nHe set down his teacup and rose to stand behind the furious Zhao Yan.\n\"Qing Lan, don't bother talking sense into me. I'll beat this disappointing son of mine dead if I have to!\"\n\"Zhao, spare us the tragic-father act. The young master's coming-of-age has passed, yet he's mediocre in study, weak in physique, plain in appearance — and he can't even manage the basic incense rites an incense warden must command. Everyone knows this. Even if you beat him to death here and now, it won't alter the Qing House's decision.\"\n\"I'm not—\"\nSmack.\nAnother slap landed hard across the boy's face.\n\"Quiet, you disgrace of a son!\"\nZhao Yan bowed his head, dragging in several harsh breaths before forcing the words out between clenched teeth:\n\"Now that the Qings have spoken so plainly... there's nothing left to discuss.\"\n\"Naturally. Whatever the elders agreed upon years ago, our generation shapes matters to fit the present. And the present is this: the young master of the Zhao House is unfit for our lady — by every measure.\"\n\"Give us five years... no, three—\"\nQing Lan released a long, weary sigh.\n\"You truly don't take a hint, do you? Forget it. The head of our house anticipated this outcome. Here — read it.\"\nHe drew a gilt-bound red letter from his sleeve and held it out.\n\"A... betrothal letter!?\"\n\"The young heir of the Zhou family is but sixteen, yet already a reserve Court Memorial Envoy — brilliant, handsome. This is the will of the Qing House's master... and of the young lady herself.\"\n\"Zhou? That wretch... he used to trail after me like a little lapdog...\"\n\"That was then. Circumstances change. I have conveyed the decision. I'll take my leave.\"\nQing Lan turned and made for the courtyard. Zhao Yan sagged in defeat, and his disappointing son remained bowed beside him, useless and silent. Just then — white mist burst across the Zhao estate.\n\"Wh—What's happening...?\"\nSoft bands of white light tore down through the clouds. No incense burned, and yet a rich, heady fragrance spilled into the courtyard.\nSeveral Bi'an, treading upon iridescent clouds, descended from above. They passed the stunned Qing Lan, swept past the kneeling Zhao Yan — and halted before Zhao Hao. One by one, they laid the white blossoms held in their jaws into his open palm.\n\"This... this is impossible!!!\"\nQing Lan's scream scraped from his throat — but it was swallowed beneath a vast voice surging through the mind of every witness, forcing upon them the words he least wished to hear:\n\"From this moment forth — by decree — Zhao Hao is appointed High Chancellor of Loongyuan.\"",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000075"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000076 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Reeling, I drift through the boundless vault of night;
from its heights, I draw the stars down into my wine.]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000076"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000077 = {
          ContentEN = "Now, in the days when the Nine Chapters of Dafenghad only just come into being, there stood a remote mountain village at Mistwharf. In it lived a herdboy. Orphaned at a young age, he spent his days tending a small flock of lambs. When he turned thirteen, his youngest lamb went missing. As forlorn as the little creature itself, the boy treasured it above all the rest — so he set out at once, following its tiny hoofprints.\nBut misfortune struck: the boy slipped and tumbled into a ravine. Half-conscious, he felt sweet dew touch his lips and warm dawnlight press against his eyelids. When he came to, he found a cluster of spirits beside him — shaped like dewdrops, gleaming with a glassy sheen — surely the beings known as the Wishen.\nSeeing him stir, the Wishen squeaked in fright and scattered into the bushes, their heads and tails all in a fluster — a most comical sight. Only after some time did they creep back, each carrying a little cup rolled from leaves. They had gathered dew to save him.\n\"Was it you who saved me?\"\nTheir little leader nodded shyly and held out the cup of dew.\nThe boy said, \"Thank you for helping me. May I come along with you?\"\nThe spirits gladly agreed. They picked wild fruit and gathered honey, using flowers as cups and leaves as plates, and they played with the boy all through the day in the forest.\nFrom that day on, no one knew where the solitary boy had gone. Only from the white chrysanthemums on the mountainside would soft laughter drift out from time to time — so people say the boy and the Wishen are still living happily in a hidden fairy haven.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000077"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000078 = {
          ContentEN = "It is said that north of Mistwharf, hidden within the mists of the inner sea, stands the Crouching-Loong Pillar, where a fiend named Zhuyin is said to lurk.\nThe baleful miasma that seeps across the lands of Huaxu is believed to rise from this very place. People shrink from its evil and dare not draw near. Yet if we quail and turn aside, how shall Huaxu ever regain its clear winds and broad, bright heavens? Thus I took leave of my family and, with fifteen able youths, set out for the Pillar to uncover the truth — hoping to be the first to lift the blight of Zhuyin.\nBreaking through the thunderous mist, we reached the cliff and at last beheld it in full. Shaped like a pine, rising straight into the clouds, it is the Crouching-Loong Pillar — and the name is no falsehood. Circling it several times, we found a stone ledge where we could bring the boat ashore.\nJagged rocks lay everywhere, all dark as ink. For miles we saw no green. Cloud swirled; thunder did not cease. Truly, as the tales say, this was a place steeped in ill omen. The limbs of my companions trembled, some near to fleeing. Thus we sent back the three who were each their family's only son. The remaining thirteen pressed on.\nAfter half a day's climb, a baleful beast rose from the mire — a creature in the shape of a Bi'an, its body wholly black — and descended upon us. Though sturdy and strong, my men could not match it. And though we lived under the Nine Chapters of Dafeng, even Lord Argent's protection could not reach us here; thus we took refuge within a cavern. Lin's firstborn, Jianchen, stepped before us and met his end — alas for him!\nThe higher we climbed, the keener the cold grew; no robe nor fur could fend it. Ten miles farther on, we reached a stone terrace ringed with steps raised over the very void. Looking up, we glimpsed a palace hanging upside down above the clouds, bound fast by more than ten chains of cold iron — an awe-striking sight. I fear it was a dwelling of the celestials, seized and fettered by the fiend.\nThree days we remained there, fearful from dawn to dusk. Water and food were nearly spent, and the fiendish beasts hunted without rest. One youth, half-delirious, murmured that he had glimpsed a black loong surging through the gloom — its head and tail unseen — much like Taihao. The others sharply silenced him, saying that Taihao has guarded Huaxu for ages and must not be likened to some baleful shadow. Yet after much thought, I do not share their certainty.\nSurveying the place, its layout seemed more a cage — and more still an altar. In youth I read widely, and learned that Huaxu holds the dual in highest regard: Heaven and Earth, yin and yang, clear and turbid. Thus, if there are two divinities, it would not be beyond reason.\nWe have been trapped here for more than half a month. The way back is lost; no path can be found. Only two of the youths remain. Thus I set down this record: should anyone find it, carry it to Haojing, and make known the truth to all our folk.\n\n(The pages that follow appear to list the names of the party, but the writing is too worn to read.)",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000078"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000079 = {
          ContentEN = "• Silk — 10,000 bolts (Delivered)\n• Cloudgossamer — 5,000 bolts (Delivered)\n• Crackle-glazed porcelain — 1,000 sets (Due in 3 days)\n• Dukang Liquor, Five-Year — 200 crates (Out of stock)",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000079"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000080 = {
          ContentEN = "I have tried every method I can recall to treat my little sister's malady. Art of Ephemerality... the older prescriptions... even those foreign medicaments I bought off the Starweavers... and at last — even that which should not be touched, ■■■...\n \nAnd still there are people who ask why I persist. \n\"Anyone practising the Zhuyou therapy dies young anyway,\" they say.\nNonsense... utter nonsense!\nWhy don't they go lose a brother or sister of their own before they dare utter such things!?\n \nNew preparation:\n■■■■■■\n■■■■\n■■■\n■■■■■\n■■■■■\n■■■■\nNo... no, this will not do.\nToo potent. Her body cannot withstand such a force.\nYet if I remove ■■■■■... the entire prescription loses its meaning!\nTry again.\n...\nAgain!\n...\nNext time... next time it may hold... it must...\n...\n...",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000080"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000081 = {
          ContentEN = "In Huaxu, the Earthborn (the Solarian people) and the Loongscion (the Charon people) share a single doctrine concerning life and death — an occurrence of notable rarity. In most regions of the world, these two groups diverge, at least in part, in their beliefs. Even within Elysian, a theocratic state, there remain Charon who continue to uphold older traditions.\n\nIt is known that the forebears of Huaxu were originally Charon natives of this land, who later intermarried with migrants from the \"Tower\" that arrived around Year 1010 of the Sacred Tower Era. It is therefore reasonable to infer that the distinctive cultural unity now found in Huaxu has its roots in a shared aspiration toward harmonious coexistence.\n\nPeople in Huaxu hold that upon death, one's soul proceeds to the Underworld, drinks from the waters of the River of Forgetting, severs all attachments to the present life, and is then reborn. This conception of unceasing renewal bears some resemblance to the Misty Sea traditions observed among the Charon people, yet it differs in one subtle respect: Huaxu doctrine appears to allow that the dead retain a measure of their own awareness.\n\nAccording to Huaxu tradition, if the deceased continue to harbour attachments to the living world, they may defer rebirth and take on a form known as the Wishen, remaining among the living. This should not be conflated with the conventional \"ghost\" of fantasy literature; the concept is fundamentally distinct. Within Huaxu culture, Wishen are commonly portrayed as friendly, playful, and gentle beings who aid the living and engage with them in lighthearted ways, rather than instilling fear or causing harm.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000081"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000082 = {
          ContentEN = "...\n\nNo. 24: Si\nThe character Si encompasses both reflective thought and quiet longing. It combines readily with other naming elements to express particular aspirations — for instance, Siyan (\"thoughtful words\"), Sigui (\"yearning to return\"), or Jingsi (\"quiet contemplation\"). It is also a frequent choice in generational names.\n\n...\n\nNo. 11: Feng\nFeng, the same character appearing in the Nine Chapters of Dafeng, carries a dignified, commanding air and traditionally reflects parents' hopes for future achievement. In recent years, however, younger parents have favoured it for its literal meaning — \"wind\" — wishing their child might grow freely, like a passing breeze.\n\n...\n\nNo. 5: Yu\nYu, meaning \"jade,\" evokes an image of a child cherished like the stone itself — gentle, refined, and precious. In Huaxu prosody, Yu is not a level-tone syllable, so pairing it with a level-tone character gives the name a smoother, more harmonious sound.\n\n...\n\nNo. 1: Liu\nLiu draws on imagery of flowing water and carries a calm, philosophical resonance. It appears in several classical Huaxu expressions originally featuring the character Liu — such as \"enduring as a long river\" and \"following what is good as naturally as water flows\". It has long been a well-loved character, but its recent surge owes much to Zhiliu, the current High Chancellor of Loongyuan. Since her appointment, not only has Liu risen sharply in popularity, but several visually similar Huaxu-script characters have also seen a remarkable uptick in use.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000082"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000083 = {
          ContentEN = "Loan Contract — Year X of Dafeng\n\nOn the Xth day of the Xth month in Year X of Dafeng, She Yuqing borrowed ten thousand copper coins from Li Laoqian, with interest accruing at one hundred per month.\nRepayment of both principal and interest is due by the Xth day of the Xth month. After that date, the total amount owed — principal and interest — shall be eligible for triple compensation..\n\nWitnessed by Lord Argent, and in accordance with the teachings of the Nine Chapters of Dafeng, both parties hereby establish this contract and seal it with their fingerprints as proof.\n\nSignatories:\nShe Yuqing\nLi Laoqian\n\nIssued in Huaxu, on the Xth day of the Xth month in Year X of Dafeng.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000083"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000084 = {
          ContentEN = "Rite of Divine Veneration\n\nThe Argent Incense joins Heaven, Earth, mortals, and the divine, bearing the grace bestowed by Lord Argent. Through its rising smoke, we offer our reverence.\nLord Argent is all-seeing and cares not for the number of sticks; sincerity alone reaches the divine. Worshipers may receive three offering sticks each day from the shrine.\n\nWhen offering incense, observe the following essentials:\n1. Purity of Body and Mind\nBefore the rite, attire shall be proper, and both body and mind kept clean and composed.\n2. Proper Kindling\nHold the incense with the tips downward to take flame. Once all three sticks are fully kindled, take them properly in hand.\n3. Correct Bearing\nWith the incense burning, hold it in both hands and stand upright with your head slightly bowed. The tips shall remain level and must never be directed towards another person.\n4. The Three Reverent Bows\n• First Bow: Before the holy image of Lord Argent, bow with head lowered — in reverence for the Nine Chapters of Dafeng, which uphold order.\n• Second Bow: Bow again — in reverence to Lord Argent, whose grace reaches all living beings.\n• Third Bow: Bow once more — in reverence to Bi'an, the divine beast who discerns right from wrong.\nWhen the rite is complete, set the sticks upright in the censer, then withdraw with measured, reverent steps.\n\nIncense is offered not for protection alone, but also in gratitude for divine grace. In our daily conduct, we shall uphold the precepts of the Nine Chapters of Dafeng and cultivate virtue — for therein lies true reverence.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000084"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000085 = {
          ContentEN = "...\nCase Ten — A Nuisance Neighbour, Settled with The Compendium of Verbal Stratagems\n\nA few years back, a man named Zhao Da came to me with a complaint. His new neighbour, Zhang San, had been pounding on his door before dawn each day, insisting that Zhao had \"sung at midnight and wouldn't let him sleep\". I'd known Zhao Da for years. He hauls cargo on the docks all day; the moment he gets home, he's out cold. The man sleeps like a stone — midnight singing wasn't even remotely possible. But Zhang San stuck to his story. Each day he came earlier, and louder. At last, one dawn when Zhao stumbled out half-asleep and thoroughly fed up, he opened the door and threw a punch. The Nine Chapters of Dafeng say: \"Heaven's great virtue bears the name of Life; To wound or kill — this must not be done.\" By the code, Zhao Da had to be punished.\n Still, Zhang San had started the trouble, and that had to be dealt with as well. Reasoning proved useless, and brawling was out of the question. So I lent Zhao a book a friend once gave me — The Compendium of Verbal Stratagems — and told him to study it. The next morning, Zhang arrived as usual, shouting. Zhao simply stood inside the threshold and replied with the book's four stock phrases: \"LMAO\", \"Copypasta\", \"Bootlicking\", and \"Seething\". Within moments, Zhang was shaking with rage and actually raised a fist to strike — and Zhao quietly pulled back his sleeve, showing the fresh welt from his punishment the day before. Zhang froze, went pale, and backed away. He came again the next morning. Zhao answered in exactly the same way. After a few days of this, Zhang San stopped coming altogether.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000085"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000086 = {
          ContentEN = "Mistwharf\n Step off the boat, and the first thing you'll see is Mistwharf. For the people of Huaxu, this stretch of land runs long — from Starwharf in the south to the Gritblight Expanse in the north.\n\nStarwharf\nWhen I meet visitors arriving in Huaxu for the first time, I always tell them not to rush inland. Linger on the quay a moment: hawkers call without pause, hardworking Huaxu porters haul crates from river barges, and Starweavers trade stories with friends from distant lands. There are few places more full of life.\n\nEverSpring\nNear the roots of the great tree lies EverSpring — another stop not to be missed. In Huaxu, everyone knows what the name stands for: to the Starweavers, it is the realm's largest guild; to visitors, a trove of rare and wondrous wares. Even if you plan to buy nothing, the towering statue of Lord Argent at the gate alone is worth the journey.\n\nTwilight Ravine\n Few from Huaxu ever venture here. Set deep within the wilderness, the Twilight Ravine is said to be the resting place of Wishen — beings other lands might call \"ghosts\". These gentle spirits are best left undisturbed.\nAs a child, I once lost my way here and stumbled upon a vast pagoda tree, its gnarled branches aglow with a soft light. I followed the shimmer to a small island at the heart of a lake, where unfamiliar voices gently led me back to the path. Looking back now, that faint glimmer must have been the Spring of Yu, hidden deep within the Ravine.\n\nHall of Ephemerality\nThe Hall of Ephemerality is Fu family's ancestral hall of healing, devoted for generations to the Zhuyou method. Should you fall ill while travelling here, this is the place to seek aid. The healer cherishes silence: whether you come for treatment or merely pass through the apricot grove beyond, please keep your voice low, that her quietude may remain undisturbed.\n\nCrowsong Hollow\nWhen I was little, my parents would tell me the tale of the Battle of Shuli at bedtime — how the Xuanheng Sect and the Jishu clashed upon this plain, leaving not a soul standing. But when I asked what they had fought for, they never had an answer.\nNow, under Taihao's protection, Huaxu has known peace for generations. The rusted remnants of Mechapuppets lie scattered across the plain, worn by wind and time — a silent draw for scholars from far and wide.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000086"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000087 = {
          ContentEN = "[Ju]\n\nThe Starweavers trace their origins to Ju — the wise sovereign who first founded Huaxu.\n\nIn the wake of establishing the Jishu Sect, Ju journeyed south to the Sacred Tower in search of a means to avert calamity. Upon his return, he brought with him those wearied by war, and together they set about restoring their homeland. In those days, the land was in need of renewal on every front. To supply what each province lacked, folk began to trade across the realm — and so began the craft that would one day define the Starweavers.\n\nToday, trade routes span the breadth of the continent, and wherever such paths may lead, the Starweavers are found. We cross mountains and rivers, travelling far beyond borders — not in pursuit of profit alone, but to bind together the many threads of civilisation. For wherever a merchant treads, there too travel the craft of the artisan and the learning of distant lands. From the meeting of these sparks, the flame of human endeavour endures.\n\n...",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000087"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000088 = {
          ContentEN = "[ID] L-W-01384\nSubsea Investigation of the Luca Temple Complex\n[Project Summary]\nThis project proposes an investigation of the trench west of the Luca Isles, commonly referred to as Andimin's Broken String. Recent, credible intelligence suggests the presence of an extensive architectural complex at considerable depth — a discovery of great significance, given that no known technology currently permits large-scale construction more than 500 metres beneath the sea. Such a finding calls into question the long-held belief that the Luca artists' reverence for a subsea deity was mere fevered fancy; their object of worship may, in fact, have had a material foundation. Despite the severe technical challenges posed by current diving limitations, we seek authorisation to conduct a preliminary survey. A successful probe may yield vital evidence concerning Luca religious practice and clarify its connection to the region's distinctive artistic tradition.\n[Schedule]\nEarly March to late June (scheduled to avoid the king-tide period)\n[Budget]\n1.217 million silver coins (see appendix)\n[Decision]\nApproved.\nComments:\n1. Ensure all personnel carry Mind-Purifying Powder and submit them for regular cross-checks to confirm continued mental stability.\n2. Keep all storage rooms properly dehumidified. I have no desire to find yet another room reeking of mould.\n\n[ID] A-C-02577\nExcavation of a Burial Site from the Great Migration\n[Project Summary]\nRecent engagements between the Empire's 8th Legion and Aethyrie resistance forces have uncovered an unrecorded ancient burial site. Preliminary assessment by local personnel suggests that the remains likely belonged to resistance fighters who fell during the Great Migration. Although all textiles and paper artefacts have deteriorated beyond recovery, osteological analysis and the surviving metal objects are expected to yield information of academic value.\n[Schedule]\nImmediate deployment recommended; estimated duration approximately two months.\n[Budget]\n104,000 silver coins (see appendix)\n[Decision]\nApproved.\nComments:\n1. Select personnel capable of handling combat if necessary. If additional protection is required, increase the budget and hire external security.\n2. Coordinate with Arcadia Academy for conservation and identification work. Finalise arrangements with them directly.\n\n[ID] H-N-03474\nHistorical Verification of the Founding of Hyperborea\n[Project Summary]\nThe Icelake region in Hyperborea is approaching its annual thaw, creating an optimal window for lake-bed investigation. With newly acquired underwater survey equipment, the Society is now equipped to examine areas that were previously inaccessible.\n[Schedule]\nJune to August\n[Budget]\n129,000 silver coins (see appendix)\n[Decision]\nPostponed.\nComments:\nThe Hyperborean Empire remains unstable, and the Forsakens appear to be preparing another strike that may reach Icelake. Do not proceed until the area is secure.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000088"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000089 = {
          ContentEN = "...\n\n※ Blind Draw\nPut each item into one of my specially made Lucky Eggs — they all look exactly the same. Customers won't know what's inside until they pick one and crack it open. They say it makes shopping far more exciting — and the thrill of landing something rare is twice as sweet.\nWhat if someone gets too lucky? Even better! That just makes them want to draw again... and again!\nAnd their big win makes great word-of-mouth — the kind that draws in even more curious customers. Either way, Lunocarp can't lose!\n\n※ Guaranteed Prize\nMake sure that after a customer opens a certain number of Lucky Eggs, they're guaranteed to get at least one rare. Sounds like a loss, right? Not at all — it puts them at ease. They'll think, \"Worst case, I'll just buy enough Eggs to hit the guarantee.\"\n And on their way there? They almost always open a few extras without even realising it!\n\n※ Limited-Time Offer\nFor a short, special window, sell Lucky Eggs that might contain a particular item. Once that \"miss it and it's gone\" feeling kicks in, customers will scramble to buy as many as they can!\n\n※ Partnered Prizes / Collab Prizes\nTeam up with someone influential and release a special prize together. Their fans will come running to buy my Lucky Eggs too — sales will soar!\nAnd if I throw in a Limited-Time Offer on top of that? Boom — even greater impact!\nAs for my very first collab... of course it has to be with the Demon King from Hyperborea! It'll sell like hotcakes!",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000089"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000090 = {
          ContentEN = [[
To all residents of Mistwharf:

In light of the Scourge of Zhuyin, contamination within the Gritblight Expanse continues to intensify and is now spreading outward. Sable Bi'an have been frequently sighted in the surrounding regions and pose a grave threat to public safety. Effective immediately, EverSpring will suspend all overland trade from Mistwharf to the interior that passes through designated high-risk zones.

This measure has been submitted to the Ministry of Fortune for approval. All residents are required to comply with this directive and must not endanger their lives in pursuit of trivial profit.

For enquiries or unresolved matters, please contact EverSpring.]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000090"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000091 = {
          ContentEN = "Mr Lin,\n\nThank you for your letter, especially amidst your many responsibilities. I am keeping well. Though I can no longer journey by sea, the work of handling goods still grants me faint glimpses of distant tales — a small comfort, but a comfort nonetheless. The ornaments you brought back from Luca are most intriguing. I shall examine them carefully and seek out suitable buyers.\n\nMy mother's condition has shown no improvement, and my two children are still very young. I cannot, in good conscience, leave the household entirely in my wife's care, and so I must once again decline your generous invitation. During my years with the caravan, you offered me much guidance; even after I left, you continued to welcome me back with the same kindness. I remain deeply grateful — and cannot help feeling a measure of shame for having disappointed you so often. May your journey be smooth, and may fortune grant you auspicious encounters along the way.\n\nI have one further request: if your route happens to pass through Icelake in Hyperborea, please convey my regards to a young Loongscion woman named Jessica. She is about my younger sister's age, and should now be in her finest years. We became acquainted when our caravan last visited the city, and our parting was not an easy one. I had hoped we might meet again before long, yet the years have slipped quietly by. If you would be willing to deliver a few small gifts to her as an apology, I would be most grateful.\n \n With my warmest wishes for your health and peace,\n Jiayan",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000091"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000092 = {
          ContentEN = "Rotten — down to the marrow.\nThat was the first thought that struck me the day I stepped into the black market, decades ago.\n Wreckage, refuse, and collapsed masonry lay strewn across the western shore. The air reeked of rot and the acrid burn of cheap incense. From the rubble's shadow, cold, hollow eyes watched the lone outsider in their midst — me.\nTheir gaze pleased me more than I cared to admit. In it, I glimpsed a hunger that mirrored my own.\nThey were the castoffs the Nine Chapters of Dafeng could never tame — refuse long abandoned by the world, shrinking from the sun and clinging to the shade. Some had lost an arm, others a leg — each bearing the wounds Bi'an leaves upon those who defy the Admonitions. Yet their eyes still tracked every newcomer with ravenous patience, lying in wait for the moment the unwary drifted too close — ready to wring out every last scrap of worth to survive another dawn, no matter how dear the cost.\nSplendid. Truly splendid. No words of praise could ever do justice to such a place — a paradise pulsing with its own savage rhythm.\nAnd I was quietly pleased.\nFor all the Nine Chapters' long schooling, each era preserves a gratifying number who never relinquish the one quality that matters most.\nGreed — or, if you insist on a more civilised term, desire. So long as that primal instinct endures, even ants may strip an elephant to the bone.\nWith a little tending, this soil — so dense with desire — would bear a forbidden fruit Huaxu could not afford to ignore.\nI doubt there is a sweeter satisfaction.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000092"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000093 = {
          ContentEN = "Securing materials, outfitting the shopfronts, bringing merchants to heel — these initial steps in quickening the market required little effort on my part. Such matters, after all, are easily settled with coin.\nThe true work lay in the second step: cultivating recognition. Without that, nothing stands — nothing built on shifting sand ever does.\nWe had to teach people that the black market was no furtive haven for stolen goods and contraband, but — in their minds — the very emblem of possibility.\nWhat others sold, we sold.\nWhat others dared not, we sold — and openly.\nRarities and exotic beasts. Paintings and private hoards. Weapons, armaments, secrets, intelligence — even lives.\nThe Nine Chapters, in the end, are an ageing relic — a code concerned only with what must not be done. Turn it on its head, and all that lies unspoken becomes permissible: a broad freedom upon which the market could flourish.\nConsider an example. Some hatreds end only in blood. A clean settling of scores invites Bi'an's censure. And yet... a hired hand? A carefully engineered ruin? The slow, social suffocation of a life?\nThe market need not take every commission, nor do I urge anyone to twist the code to their advantage. The point is simple: for a price, we offer a path towards long-cherished desires — and that is what wins the world's recognition of our reach.\nSome will deem it wrong — even evil. Yet without evil, virtue has no meaning. Such is the creed my people of the market claim as their own.\nOnce those two steps were set in place, vitality followed of its own accord. From that moment, the market began, at last, to move along its rightful course.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000093"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000094 = {
          ContentEN = "I once read of the so-called \"Rule of One Hundred and Fifty\": that beyond this number, friction multiplies and cooperation begins to decay.\nThe market crossed that threshold by its third year. From that point on, recognition alone no longer sufficed; an iron rule was required.\n Unlike my other ventures, the market keeps but a single law:\nThose who place a wager must accept its result.\nTrade here, and you gain the leverage to contend with those above you. Stand loyal to me, and the fetters of want fall away. Only one boundary limits privilege: take no pride in victory, and voice no complaint in defeat.\nI turn a blind eye to profit that strays beyond conventional morality — but whoever pursues it must shoulder the risks as well.\nAs for those unequal to loss — at best: reclamation of assets, expulsion from the market, the withdrawal of shelter. At worst... such matters need not be set down in a memoir; I would not burden Huaxu's children with unwholesome dreams.\n\nThe essence of our rise has been spoken. What follows may serve merely as after-dinner anecdotes.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000094"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000095 = {
          ContentEN = "The black market has never been a cage without exit. Over the decades, there were always those who chose to leave and pursue another path.\n Such intent has always pleased me; it affirms that my long-standing counsel has taken root: follow your desire.\nTo see them through that first and hardest step, I provided capital, manpower, and the necessary connections. Where desire is pursued, no investment is ever wasted.\nSome did fail. Yet so long as the desire driving them did not wither, the market's favour remained with them.\nSome succeeded, found their footing across various trades, and returned a portion of their gains to the market.\nI ask no return; their offerings often hold little weight for me. But long distances are made of small steps, and even a modest force, rightly placed, may turn the course at a crucial moment.\nThus, through these ties, the market's branches spread through every quarter of Huaxu.\nThe sapling I planted has, under many hands, grown into a great tree — beyond any hope of uprooting.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000095"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000096 = {
          ContentEN = "Alchemist's Journal (Ⅰ)\n\n(A journal left behind in Glevum Pit. The tone suggests it might belong to a scholar.)\n...\nApril 16\nWe've found a peculiar <H>yellow crystal cluster</> in this pit. Judging by its texture, it's neither the legendary Crystallo of Stella said to be hidden deep in mountain caves, nor the Phoxene left behind in Glevum Pit. Instead, it appears to be a completely new, undiscovered mineral.\n...\nApril 18\nBob was astonished to find that these yellow crystal clusters, which emitted a dim glow, had <H>grown larger</>. They had expanded from the size of a palm to that of a low shrub.\n...\nApril 20\n...The yellow crystal clusters had again \"grown larger\" and turned into spiky crystals that blocked our way, as if they were alive. Fortunately, they were fragile and could be easily broken with a small amount of <H>gunpowder</>.\nBob hypothesized that these crystals were not minerals but some kind of secretion from Filthoids — even a \"subordinate form\" of it. Over time, they expanded like a sponge soaked in water. Although Bob was well-versed in the Biology of Filthoids, this was still a bold assumption.\n...\nApril 21\nTo verify Bob's hypothesis, we decided to venture deeper into the pit. If his guess is right, there must be a special Filthoid <H>parent form</> down there, different from the ones we've seen in the wild.\n...",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000096"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000097 = {
          ContentEN = "Alchemist's Journal (Ⅱ)\n\n(A journal left behind in Glevum Pit, stained dark red. The handwriting matches the earlier journal found. The latter part is extremely messy, as if it was scribbled in frantic haste.)\n\n...\nApril 23\nAs Bob predicted, we found that special Filthoid deep in the pit, an area once deemed the \"Forbidden Zone\" of Glevum Pit.\nIt measures over 20 meters in length, making it the largest Filthoid I've ever encountered. It resembles a skeleton, with yellow crystals continuously \"growing\" from the ends of its limbs—we can be certain that it is the \"parent form\" of the yellow crystals we've seen along the way.\nWe've decided to stay hidden and continue observing this creature.\n...\nApril 26\nWe've found that, compared to other wandering Filthoids, this one has a distinct peculiarity in addition to its larger size: It seems to have a particular fascination with <H>glowing minerals</>. It often wanders to abandoned mining pits, repeatedly performing the same digging motions. Whenever it unearths glowing minerals—such as Phoxene fragments from the mines or the Silverite from the mountains—it grows yellow crystals from the tips of its limbs to encase these luminous rocks.\nBob is extremely intrigued by this habit: Its behavior of excavating, collecting, and encapsulating the glowing objects doesn't seem to be a mere phototaxis. This series of actions appears to be something more akin to \"conscious\" behavior.\nIs it possible that Filthoids retain some form of consciousness similar to that of humans? If this hypothesis is proven to be true, it would completely overturn the existing research findings in the field of the Biology of Filthoids.\n...\nApril 27\nWe made a grave mistake.\nIn an attempt to further verify its behavioral patterns, Bob tried to provoke it by stealing from its \"collection\" and observing its reaction.\nHowever, we severely underestimated its reaction speed. It lunged at us with a speed we had never witnessed before, attempting to tear us, the unwelcome intruders, into pieces.\nThough Bob bought me enough time to escape its lair, I knew all too well that the injuries I had sustained were fatal. I would never make it out of this abandoned mine alive.\nIf anyone finds this journal, I implore you to take it to The Spathatoi Academy in Dismania. The study of this Filthoid—whom we named \"the Crystalophile\"—could bring about a monumental shift in the Biology of Filthoids as we know it.\nKnowledge should not be buried with us in this pit.\n\nInes Morley, an Alchemist",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000097"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000098 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Wake up, young man. Keep your feet on solid ground. There is no such thing as gaining without graft. Every master of the Wuqiongzang Sect earned their skill through years of hard, honest training, and nothing else.
 
Stop swallowing nonsense like Loong in the Sky; that trashy tale has led enough gullible lads astray. Don't fool yourself into thinking fortune will drop into your lap and carry you to the top with no effort of your own.
 
Take this small sum and get yourself looked at. A fall from a cliff is no small matter. From now on, train steadily. Keep your head clear. Stop daydreaming, and stop chasing shortcuts that lead nowhere!]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000098"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000099 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Prepare a red calf; burn it to the south, in sacrifice unto High Heaven.
Take sheep and swine, and bury them to the north, in sacrifice unto the Bounteous Earth.
To the east, offer unto the Sun; to the west, unto the Moon.
Establish the pit and altar, and offer separate rites to the Four Quarters and the Four Seasons.
At the time of sacrifice, the Three Masters of Rites shall officiate.
Whoso Taihao favours shall bear the Lunarose Incense and stand at the centre, to make offering unto the Divine Lord.
Whoso is broadly learned shall bear the Sumei Incense and stand to the left, to make offering unto the ancestors.
Whoso excels in the myriad arts shall bear the Aurumwood Incense and stand to the right, to make offering unto the Spirits of Land and Grain.
Let no garments be adorned with deep indigo or dark crimson; let neither red nor purple be worn as inner raiment.
Speak not during the rite. When the offerings are complete, keep watch over the sacrificial fire for the space of a quarter-hour.
Let sacrificial food not be kept overnight; should it be kept beyond three days, it shall not be used.

Note: These regulations are intricate and lavish, and do not accord with the temper of Huaxu today. They are set down here only as a record of former times and ought not to be restored.]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000099"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000100 = {
          ContentEN = "...\n\nIn sum, Huaxu's Grand Incense Rite is the country's most important recurring festival. Its ritual form has shifted over the centuries, yet the stages of that evolution are surprisingly well attested, each aligning with Huaxu's economic, technological, and artistic development at the time.\n\nA plausible hypothesis is that the festival began as a large-scale rite unique to Huaxu. Over time, it adapted to the needs and conditions of each era, shedding many elements while preserving its essential core — through which the people of Huaxu give shape to a distinctive cultural identity.\n\nThe \"divinities\" worshipped by the people of Huaxu are not mere symbols. They exist, and they influence the ecology within Huaxu's borders in concrete, observable ways. I have personally witnessed the descent of the deity called Taihao during the Grand Incense Rite, and the experience left me profoundly shaken.\n\n...\n\nI am beginning to suspect a flaw in my earlier line of inquiry. I once assumed the Grand Incense Rite resembled festivals in other nations: cultural practices that grew out of commemoration and sacrifice, preserving certain customs as tradition. Ordinarily, such customs become simpler and more secularised as time advances.\n\nYet after examining every extant record of the Rite within Huaxu, I found that its central custom — the act of incense offering itself — has remained unchanged in both materials and quantity for nearly a thousand years of recorded history.\n\nGiven the manifest presence of \"divine\" power within Huaxu's territory, I have reason to believe the Rite is not merely a cultural or political form of worship. It may have a distinct functional purpose. Put plainly: in Huaxu, and only in Huaxu, a rite that exchanges offerings for a deity's protection appears to function as an actual transaction.\n\nBut why would Taihao require these incenses? And what, in fact, is Taihao? No comparable records exist elsewhere in Atlasia, and the incense used for such offerings is produced only within Huaxu... Incense found only here — and a \"god\" found only here that feeds upon it? The symmetry is too neat. It feels less like coincidence than design.\n\n...\n\nThis functional reading suggests an intriguing possibility. Perhaps Huaxu's so-called \"divinities\" are not the exalted beings of popular imagination at all, but something closer to a machine — an apparatus that regulates the environment within its sphere of influence. It may even affect, or outright control, nearby Filthoids, pressing them into service as a blunt tool of enforcement. In that case, the Grand Incense Rite is less a ceremony than a scheduled procedure: refuelling this immense \"machine\" with what Huaxu calls \"incense\", at fixed intervals and in fixed amounts.\n\nIf this conjecture proves true, it would imply that, a thousand years ago, the people of Huaxu had mastered a technology capable of creating a \"god\". Should such a technology be deciphered, the Nine Chapters of Dafeng might be extended across Atlasia; or the mechanism's field of influence might be scaled down to the individual, enabling the creation of specialised protective devices.\n\nYet if such a technology truly exists, why does it function only within Huaxu's borders? Even the Starweavers have left no record of employing similar techniques beyond Huaxu’s border. What, then, is the crucial factor that imposes this limitation?\n\n...",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000100"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000101 = {
          ContentEN = [[
...

The leaf had long since turned yellow, yet the traces of ink upon it remained clear. Yin Qing studied it, gave a long, wistful sigh, and after a moment allowed himself a faint smile.
"I once wished to roam the world," he said to Feng, "yet my father and mother would not permit it. So I wrote upon this green leaf and set it adrift with the current, to ease my longing. Now that you have chanced upon it, carry it with you on your travels, that my wish may journey beside you."
Feng was silent for a long while. Then he replied,
"Why should we not travel together?"

...

When they reached the Lin River, Feng was stricken with a grave illness and lay bedridden. Qing said, "Rest now, and recover your strength. I shall remain here until you are well; then we shall set out again."
But Feng would not agree. He drew forth the withered leaf and pressed it into Qing's hand.
"Take this leaf," he said. "Go far. Let it be as though we still travel side by side."

...

Qing travelled far and wide, and in time he filled an entire chest with his verses. Wherever he went, he won admirers who pressed him to remain, yet he declined them all.
In his later years, he returned once more to the familiar banks of the Lin. Before Feng's grave, he buried the withered leaf in the soil and laid fresh green leaves above it.
Upon a leaf he inscribed two lines:

"Green leaves lie upon your grave;
The ache in my heart drifts with the stream."]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000101"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000102 = {
          ContentEN = "Hall of Adjudication\n\nIn the early days of the Nine Chapters of Dafeng, the Hall of Adjudication played a far more prominent role. Its original purpose, however, has long since faded from common memory and is now known only to a handful of dedicated historians.\n\nBeneath the Hall, a bridge-like span arches across the air like a rainbow. Legend holds that Lord Argent sometimes soars through the heavens and listens, unmoving, to the clamour of the mortal world — proof that Heaven heeds the people, and that divine law watches over all.\n\nToday, the Hall stands as Huaxu's political centre. It rises against the clouds, visible from nearly anywhere in Haojing — much as the Nine Chapters of Dafeng stand before the people: clear, enduring, and impossible to ignore.\n\n⸻\n\nZesheng Pharmacy\n\nIn former times, the people of Huaxu turned to healers of the Zhuyou clan when illness struck. In later ages, physicians devoted to pharmacology emerged, along with apothecaries to prepare and dispense medicines.\n\nZesheng Pharmacy is Haojing's most respected establishment of its kind. It is said that its founder was once herself a Zhuyou, yet she abandoned those arts in favour of pharmacology, elevating the discipline to new heights. Rare herbs and medicinal ingredients are supplied here, and the pharmacy is renowned for its proprietary prescriptions. It also offers seasonal wellness regimens to help preserve one's health.\n\nIf thirst overtakes you while wandering the streets, do stop by — herbal tonics are served free to the public.\n\n⸻\n\nYoulai Alley\n\nSome popular Huaxu tales claim that the dead may linger in a form known as Wishen. Unlike the fearful portrayals of spirits and ghosts found in other lands, Huaxu's Wishen are remembered in legend as gentle, even endearing beings. Though they may occasionally cause minor disturbances, it is often out of kindness, as if to guide the living along their path.\n\nHaojing folklore speaks of a place called Youlai Alley, where Wishen with unfinished attachments are said to gather. Most dismiss such claims as idle fancy. Yet there are whispers that if one follows the winding lanes of the eastern quarter long enough, one might, by chance, stumble into that mysterious domain...\n\n⸻\n\nJiaolan Village\n\nIf Haojing's bustle begins to wear on you, head west. Jiaolan Village offers a quiet refuge from the city's noise. This small settlement lies on Haojing's western outskirts, close enough to reach on foot.\n\nHuaxu's incense culture stretches back into antiquity, and Jiaolan Village is regarded as its birthplace. Though the craft of incense-making has long since spread across the land, and Jiaolan is no longer the only place to find it, it remains home to the largest incense-drying grounds in the region surrounding Haojing. When the aromatic plants come into bloom, villagers harvest them, rinse them clean, and lay them out beneath the sun. On fair days, when a gentle breeze passes through, the fragrance carries for miles, creating an atmosphere at once ethereal and intoxicating.\n\nBefore you depart, should you wish to bring home a thoughtful gift, the villagers' handcrafted incense makes an excellent choice: elegant in scent, and rich in Huaxu's distinctive cultural character.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000102"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000103 = {
          ContentEN = "Top Dining Pick: Bazhen Pavilion\n\nIn last month's reader poll, Bazhen Pavilion beat the runner-up by nearly a hundred votes — clearly a crowd favourite.\n\nOur rule is simple: we don't recommend what we haven't tried. So we went in as ordinary diners and ordered the signature dishes. The fan-favourite Bazhen Tofu really is a standout: silky-soft, layered with flavour, and finished with a scatter of corn kernels — looks as good as it tastes.\n\nMistwharf Vinegar Fish is a classic, but the sharp vinegar tang isn't for everyone, especially with freshwater fish. A couple of us left it unfinished.\n\n...\n\nFrom Diner A: Chef Bai's cooking speaks for itself. And Chef Li isn't far behind. Word is his poems are just as good as his food.\n\nFrom Diner B: When I was young, my family always celebrated my birthday at Bazhen Pavilion... Years have gone by. Though the people who once sat beside me are no longer with me, the dishes taste the same. And perhaps that is enough.\n\nFrom Diner C: Whenever friends or relatives visit Haojing, Bazhen Pavilion is the first place I take them. That said, lately some of the new dishes feel like they're trying a little too hard to please out-of-towners. It's not quite the same as it used to be — not that there's anything wrong with catering to visitors.\n\n...",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000103"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000104 = {
          ContentEN = "\"Ah! How magnificent are the Nine Chapters of Dafeng!\nWhen chaos first gave way, the Nine Chapters were set in place;\nBy the covenant of the two divine lords, the standard was forged;\nThe Argent Loong upheld the law, and inscribed the righteous way;\nHigh it stands, vast and unshaken — never to be toppled!\n...\nGreat indeed — great indeed!\"\n\nMarginal note, in another hand: The opening and closing are predictably trite, and the middle offers nothing worth keeping. Rewrite.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000104"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000105 = {
          ContentEN = "The art of incense has changed with the ages. In antiquity, aromatic herbs were burnt to raise fragrant smoke to reach the divine; in the present age, incense is measured and its vapours harmonised, so that by this craft the Mandate of Heaven may be set in order.\n\nThe Record of Yang's Life relates that at the founding of the Nine Chapters, Yang declared: \"When the Mandate of Heaven intermingles, all courses are impeded, and what is tangled cannot easily be loosed.\" She therefore sent men to gather a myriad of aromatics and heap them at the Taixu Mausoleum, where they were burnt without cease, day and night. Yang herself sat beside the pyre. When the smoke ran thick, none could draw near; and when at last it thinned, she had vanished.\n\nThis account need not be taken on faith. Versions differ from place to place, and much has shifted with the passing of years. I compile this book not to quarrel over such tales, but to set down the incense arts of Huaxu across the span of ages. Within these pages I have gathered, as exhaustively as I could, matters of Heaven and Earth, of human affairs and the produce of the land; what was ancient is collected, and what is current is brought within its compass. It is my hope that those who come after may follow this lineage, and enrich it in their turn.\n\nThe whole consists of seventeen volumes. The first nine record the incense materials: orchid and fragrant herb, pepper and cassia, aloeswood, sandalwood, borneol and musk — ancient varieties of Huaxu — together with rare foreign aromatics once presented as tribute.\n\nMarginal note: Since the widening of the Starweavers' routes, what was once unheard-of has become obtainable.\n\nThe next four volumes treat of the uses of incense. Incense serves many ends: for offering, for wear, and for medicine. These are set out in detail below.\n\nThe final three volumes concern the art of incense blending. Though its principles may appear simple, it is in truth the most demanding of all related crafts. One must first master the preceding thirteen volumes and commit them thoroughly to memory; only then may one properly undertake this discipline.\n\nThe uses of incense are as boundless as the sea, and their subtleties are still beyond our full grasp. I have done no more than gather and preserve a handful of remarkable formulae devised by sages of old, that their methods might endure.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000105"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000106 = {
          ContentEN = "No need for the Moon Matchmaker to meddle. One red thread binds the romance. The crimson bridal sedan chair halts at the gate. Whether she wills it or no — gown on, veil down — once she mounts, the union is sealed. A hundred hands bear it onwards. The bridegroom reels, heart unmoored, as though he had glimpsed a heavenly maiden in a dream.\n\n(Offstage, a hurried reply.)\nJing (beaming): Splendid! Good luck upon good luck — three cups for that!\nMo: It's late. My lady is waiting. I'll be taking my leave.\nJing: Nonsense! You've drained more cups than I can count at the Bazhen Pavilion and never once been drunk. And now you're baulking at a single cup? You'll be the laughingstock!\nMo: Things aren't what they were. Don't take it amiss, brother.\n(Mo bows with clasped hands.)\nJing (laughing): Ha! And here I thought you were a man of mettle — turns out you go to ground the moment wine's poured.\nJing: Fine, fine. If he's that set on ducking out, no need to push it.\nMo (startled): Why would you say that? All these years of brotherhood, and you'd throw it away over one cup of wine?\nJing: Brotherhood? If that means anything to you — today's your wedding day, everyone's here to toast you. And you won't drink a single cup with us?\nAll: Hear, hear!\nJing: Some loyalty. Some brother.\nAll: Hear, hear!\nMo (sighing): Pomegranate blazes red as fire... and still, it cannot match the bitter smoke of mugwort... Ah, well.\nMo (raising his voice): Today I'll drink with you if it kills me. Gentlemen — your health!\n(All drink.)\n\nA crescent moon crests the little tower, yet its tender light falls only into the gutter. Hair dressed in vain, pipa in hand... whose ear awaits her song?",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000106"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000107 = {
          ContentEN = "A sachet to cheer up my workaholic friends\n— 1 part Ivorywood, 3 parts Vernal Prime.\nVernal Prime has a crisp, refreshing fragrance, perfect for a more \"put-together\" vibe. Ivorywood helps keep you steady when work gets stressful.\n\nA sachet to win the hearts of every stray cat\n— A small pinch of Loong Qiqi, 4 parts Ivorywood.\nThe secret is that tiny salty, fishy note in Loong Qiqi — it smells a little wild, in a way cats seem to love. Add the cosy warmth of Ivorywood, and they might just decide we're \"one of them\".\n\nA sachet to help my favourite author update faster\n— As much Frostleaf as you can manage.\nFrostleaf keeps you wide awake, so the writer I'm following can pull an all-nighter and get a chapter out. So yes: be generous... Teehee...",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000107"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000108 = {
          ContentEN = "The Ministry of Works hereby announces the following works under its charge:\n\nⅠ. Rehabilitation of Official Roads\nBy order, the city's principal roads and minor roads are to be repaired and reinforced. Roadbeds shall be strengthened and carriageways widened, easing the passage of carts and riders and improving travel throughout the city, so that the routes may run clear in all directions.\n\nⅡ. Construction of Freight-Handling Apparatus\nNew freight-handling apparatus shall be constructed, with a wrought-iron frame and precision gearing. Efficiency shall be improved and operations standardised, speeding the circulation of goods and encouraging commercial growth.\n\nⅢ. Reinforcement and Repair of the Wharves\nThe existing wharves shall undergo full inspection and repair. Riverbanks shall be reinforced, rotted timbers replaced, and additional safeguards installed, ensuring safe passage for vessels and keeping grain transport secure.\n\n...\n\nAll works shall be carried out in accordance with established codes: rooted in traditional practice, yet open to new techniques. The Ministry of Works faithfully discharges its duty, securing the foundations of the state and serving the needs of the people.\n\nThose wishing to consult the full project files may do so in the Ministry's archives.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000108"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000109 = {
          ContentEN = "In considering what the sages of old intended when they taught learning, I take it to be this: to press inquiry to its limits, and to read widely in the old and the new, so as to broaden the mind and enlarge one's view. Only then may a person judge right from wrong, weigh matters with clarity, and grasp the workings of the world — not merely memorise texts and polish fine phrases for the sake of fame, profit, and preferment.\n\nThose who study in our day have borne hardships for too long. Much of what the former worthies taught is recorded in the classics, yet remains detached from worldly affairs. Anyone with resolve ought to read deeply, reflect, and question — to examine and sift what one has learned. For if one recognises the vastness of Heaven and Earth and the complexity of affairs, and strives to look upon all things with objectivity and a mind trained to weigh matters with discernment, then the standards by which one stands in the world and conducts oneself are already within one's grasp. Why wait for others to hand them over before one can hold fast to them?\n\nAs for what I have written, it cannot guide every student, and the methods it proposes may not fully accord with the true aim of opening the mind and awakening thought. Therefore I shall no longer require it in this hall. Instead, I have selected the main principles by which the sages taught learning, set them forth as articles, and posted them here for all to see. Let all of you study them together, make their meaning clear, keep them, and hold yourselves to them. Then, in the midst of your thoughts and actions, your judgement will grow more exacting: you will deliberate with care, discern with clarity, and investigate things so as to extend your knowledge. These are, in truth, the roots of conduct in the world, and the foundations of cultivating virtue and turning towards the good.\n\nOnly by keeping to this guiding framework may one contemplate all things, and so bring forth new understanding and open new paths.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000109"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000110 = {
          ContentEN = "4 June\nFiled by: Wang Quan\nReason: I say sweet tofu pudding is best. Zhang Fu swears by the savoury kind. Only one way to settle it — a match!\n\n28 June\nFiled by: Xu Hong\nReason: I want to test a new explosive prototype. The yard is open enough for it.\n\n15 July\nFiled by: Su Yi\nReason: Lu Ye and Pei Jing want to practise with crossbows. I've been asked to keep an eye on them.\n\n16 July\nFiled by: Su Yi\nReason: Same as yesterday (the kids are really coming along!).\n\n6 August\nFiled by: Uncle Tang\nReason: Finished a new battlefield loudhailer. Need everyone to take turns singing in the yard for a sound test.\n\n20 August\nFiled by: Uncle Tang\nReason: Zhu Pu overtrained and hurt his leg. Want to test the support brace I made for him and log a few measurements.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000110"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000111 = {
          ContentEN = "I. Meowvellous Machine\n\nLogged by: Uncle Tang (love you lot, everyone in Outer Peaks!)\n\nSetup:\nSplit the kids into two groups. One got a pep talk from the Meowvellous Machine beforehand; the other didn't.\nBoth groups then read Mechanism Basics That Every Huaxu Kid Can Learn. After one stick of incense, we gave them a quiz.\n\nResult:\nSome of the \"encouraged\" kids (Zhu Pu, for instance) made the top ten. Others landed near the bottom. The kids who didn't get the pep talk (like Pei Jing) ended up somewhere in the middle.\n\nComments:\nSu Yi: The groups weren't evenly matched to begin with. The results don't prove anything.\nXu Hong: I think some of them were just scared by how the machine looks.\nKezhou: The results aren't the point. I just want to ask... what's with that signature in the \"Logged by\" line? Has Uncle Tang been breathing in workshop fumes again?\n\n\nII. Duck and Dash\n\nLogged by: Uncle Tang (still loving you lot today)\n\nSetup:\nAfter basic safety checks, we went out into the wild. On spotting a lone Sable Bi'an, give a Duck and Dash a full wind and toss it.\n\nResult:\nDidn't scare it off. If anything, it made so much noise that it drew more of them in and made them meaner.\n\nAreas to Improve:\nTry making it even louder.\n\nComments:\nSu Yi: Why not pack it with a few small charges? Like the firecrackers we set off at New Year — toss it, then leg it while it's popping.\nUncle Tang: This is meant for schoolchildren to use for self-defence on the way home. If we add anything like that, I'm worried they'll misuse it and hurt themselves.\nShen Han: Are the children all right? No one was hurt, I hope? Uncle Tang — please don't drag them into such dangerous experiments next time!",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000111"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000112 = {
          ContentEN = "Within Huaxu's system of governance, few institutions are as distinctive as the Incense Trial — an annual examination established by the Ministry of Rites to seek out promising talent among the young. Built around Huaxu's own incense culture, it proceeds in stages, each stricter than the last. Learning is tested openly; character and suitability are weighed more quietly. In time, it has become a signature feature of Huaxu's governance.\n\nShrine Trial — Timed by Smoke, Judged by Learning\nThe selection begins with the Shrine Trial, a small regional examination held throughout Huaxu. Each sitting is held at the district's Argent Loong Temple: a first-round screening, and a gesture of reverence to Lord Argent. The trial is conducted inside the temple itself, overseen by the temple's incense wardens, who are charged with keeping order and impartiality from start to finish.\nThere is no interview at this stage; the focus is purely on a candidate's learning. On the appointed day, candidates enter with their admission slips and take their seats. Writing materials are issued, but no one may touch brush to paper until permitted. Only when the supervising warden lights the designated Lunarose incense, and the candidates have completed their rite of thanks to Lord Argent, does the examination begin in earnest. The time allowed is exactly the burn of a single stick of Lunarose incense.\nThe top three from each sitting advance, and must in due course travel to Huaxu's capital, Haojing, for a harsher round of selection.\n\nDepartmental Trial — Matched to Post, Weighed in Talent and Virtue\nCandidates who reach Haojing then face the Departmental Trial, where suitability is the core concern. Unlike the Shrine Trial, which leans on written testing, this stage is not centred on the brush alone. Greater weight is placed on practical ability and how well a candidate fits the duties of the post. Interviews allow examiners to judge a candidate's speech, thinking, and character at closer range. Before sitting the trial, candidates must state their intended post, making an initial choice among the Three Ministries and their subordinate departments. The assessment shifts accordingly, depending on the functions of the chosen institution.\nThe close of the Departmental Trial marks the end of that year's Incense Trial. Once the list of successful candidates is confirmed, the Ministry of Rites summons all qualified entrants to the Hall of Adjudication to take up their appointments. Those whose talent and conduct are deemed exceptional may even win the favour of Lord Argent. In such cases, a Bi'an delivers a summons, granting leave to advance to a third stage: a final selection for the office of High Chancellor of Loongyuan.\nDuring this interval, the Hall of Adjudication enters a brief recess to prepare postings and the High Chancellor selection to follow. Meanwhile, the Hall of Incense hastens its own preparations, assembling special aromatics reserved solely for the High Chancellor's selection.\n\nAs for how the High Chancellor of Loongyuan is ultimately chosen — Huaxu has no shortage of opinions. I have only learned that an internal procedure does exist, and that it is said to be fairly comprehensive.\nFor reasons of space, however, we'll leave Loongyuan for the next issue.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000112"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000113 = {
          ContentEN = "Do you want to become High Chancellor of Loongyuan?\nLet me be specific: set aside the tangled official duties the post entails. Imagine instead that you keep the title and bask in universal admiration across Huaxu without having to lift a finger. Under those conditions, do you want it?\nI expect most answers would be: of course!\nWhich brings us to the real question: how does one actually become High Chancellor of Loongyuan?\n\nIn last issue, we touched on the Incense Trial — the examination system by which the state selects officials from among the common run of Huaxu's citizens. But what of those so-called prodigies, the dazzling geniuses beyond the common measure? Is there a special track reserved for them? Are they appointed the moment they first wail into the world?\nCertainly not.\nBecoming High Chancellor of Loongyuan is no simple matter. Most readers will have guessed correctly: those singled out do indeed receive a message from Lord Argent — delivered, of course, by a Bi'an. But don't celebrate just yet. Receiving the message does not mean you've already made it. All it gives you is a new label: Candidate for High Chancellor of Loongyuan.\nYou answer the summons and travel to Haojing. There, the sitting High Chancellor receives you with great warmth. You gather in the Hall of Adjudication and talk — perhaps of state affairs, perhaps simply of daily life. This stage has a name: \"The Trial of the Way.\"\nWhen it ends, you are escorted back to your temporary quarters at the posting station to await the next summons.\nAnd you wait.\nDays pass. You pace like a cat on a hot tin roof, opening the door every few moments to see whether someone has come with news. The good news is that someone eventually does. If they didn't, this essay would end here — and then what would you do with your evening?\n\nFilled with anticipation, you return once more to the Hall of Adjudication. You assume that since your previous examiner was the sitting High Chancellor of Loongyuan, this time you must surely meet Lord Argent themselves. But upon arrival, you notice there is no Lord Argent, nor the High Chancellor. Instead, a handful of Hall staffers hurriedly thrust an enormous stack of dossiers into your arms and then flee at once, as if the building is about to collapse.\nNow comes your task: in this sea of records, you must trace the links between designated incidents and present your own reasoned conclusions. Someone will see to your meals, your lodging, your daily needs. You, however, are strictly forbidden from cutting corners. Try to slack off and you will be struck from the candidate list without ceremony.\nThis stage is called \"The Trial of Statecraft.\"\nNaturally, you are exceptional. After what feels like years poring over records — memorising every last petty detail from the past twenty years — you finally receive word one morning: you passed. The Trial of Statecraft is complete. Next comes, supposedly, an audience with Lord Argent.\nYou are beside yourself. You rush to Qianxiu Pavilion to have a new set of robes made — something bright and showy for the occasion. How many people go their entire lives without hearing even a single true word from Lord Argent? And you — you might see Them with your own eyes. What fortune beyond measure!\nUnable to contain your excitement, you tell the shop assistant exactly why you are having clothes made. The girl takes one look at your glowing face and cannot help it: she covers her mouth and giggles.\n\"You know,\" she says, \"not every candidate gets to see Lord Argent.\"\nYou go pale. You are just about to seek a further explanation when a messenger arrives with urgent orders: report to the Hall of Adjudication at once and prepare for the Trial of the Heart. With no time to dig, you hurriedly straighten your robes and make your way back to that now-familiar place.\n\nInside, incense smoke curls through the Hall — thick enough to be seen, yet without the faintest scent. This seems odd. An attendant arrives and has you sit on a cushion with the other candidates, instructing you to meditate and regulate your breath. Suspicious but obedient, you sit. Smoke coils around your eyes and nose. Your thoughts grow heavy. Drowsiness steals over you. You think you might as well close your eyes for a moment. So you do.\nAnd you fall asleep.\nYou dream a long dream.\nIn it, you see your past: your mother cradling you, your father lifting you high above his head.\nIn it, you see your future: you succeed to the post of High Chancellor of Loongyuan, spend a lifetime wringing yourself dry for the people of Huaxu, and at last are laid to rest in the Taixu Mausoleum.\nIn it, you see others — countless faces, countless eyes fixed upon you. Questions rise, one after another, pressing and demanding. You are flustered, drenched in sweat, pushed to the brink — and then you jolt awake.\nYou look up. The incense lit at the beginning has not yet burned out.\n\nIn the end, you do not pass the selection. Presumably, your performance in the dream failed to please Lord Argent. Still, though not chosen, you ultimately join the Hall of Incense and continue serving the people of Huaxu. A happy ending all the same, wouldn't you say?\n\nAnd so, dear readers, you now know exactly how to become High Chancellor of Loongyuan. And if one of you actually does make it to High Chancellor someday... well, I trust you'll remember me, the humble author who wrote this helpful little piece. A little token of appreciation sent my way would not go amiss. Don't you think?",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000113"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000114 = {
          ContentEN = "Ⅰ. East Market: Floodwater Half-Drowns a Lord Argent Statue\n\nAfter days of relentless rain, Haojing Lake rose fast enough to spill over the East Market embankment. One shopkeeper, known locally for his devotion, kept a three-foot wooden statue of Lord Argent at his shopfront and burnt incense to it each day without fail.\nEarly one morning, a shophand named Zhang opened up the shop to sweep the threshold — only to find the water already lapping at the steps, the statue's lower half swallowed up. Startled, Zhang shouted for help, and the neighbours together hauled the image further inside.\nTongues wag: \"Lord Argent's ways are inscrutable.\" Surely this is Their doing — making plain Their displeasure at private offerings by flooding Their own image, a stark reminder to respect the seasons and Heaven's will.\n\n\nⅡ. Astonishing: Sixty-Two-Year-Old Widow Gives Birth\n\nMrs Liu of Haojing, a widow of sixty-two, has long lived alone and childless, and in her quiet days was often lonely.\nIn recent months, she complained frequently of fatigue and found her appetite greatly increased. Neighbours took it for the usual signs of age. No one expected what came after: on the nineteenth of this month, she gave birth to a baby boy — loud-voiced when he cried, bright-eyed, and fine-featured.\nWord soon reached the Hall of Founts, which sent an officer to verify the matter. Mrs Liu claims that three months ago she dreamt of a woman in blue who offered her a peach, sweet beyond telling; upon waking, she felt her body begin to change. The child bears a faint red birthmark at the waist, shaped unmistakably like a peach — proof that the dream was no mere fancy.\nStrange indeed. Such tales belong, people say, to storybooks — not to everyday life. The neighbours insist Mrs Liu has always been known for kindness, and some whisper that Heaven, in its own way, repays the virtuous.\nThe Hall of Founts has registered the child and granted assistance in grain and bolts of cloth. Friends and relations arrive daily with congratulations, and curious onlookers have been gathering in no small numbers.\n\n\nⅢ. West Market Oddity: A Dog That Hunts Rats\n\nAt the West Market eatery Baiwei House, the proprietor Li Sanlang keeps a yellow dog named Rusty to mind the place at night. He has been a good dog: quiet, steady, and has never caused trouble.\nOn the afternoon of the first of this month, the shophands were airing out dried ingredients in the sun when Rusty suddenly wouldn't stop barking. They hurried over and saw him dart to a rat hole at the base of the wall. A moment later he came back out with a grey rat clenched in his jaws, already dead.\nTaken aback, the hands reported it to Li Sanlang. Li wouldn't believe a word of it — until the next day, when he saw it with his own eyes: Rusty crouched by the hole, and the moment a rat poked its head out, he lunged. Before the day was out, he'd caught three. One regular laughed: \"The old saying compares a dog that catches mice to nosy folk who meddle in other people's business — but it's a blessing when your place is full of rats.\"\nSince then, Rusty has hunted rats by day and kept watch by night. Baiwei House, it is said, has had no trouble with rats since.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000114"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000115 = {
          ContentEN = "(The sheet has been trampled and scuffed; much of the text is blurred.)\n...\n...\n...\n\nⅡ. Unrest on the Western Outskirts — Drifters Sighted Again?\n\nIn recent days, citizens have been arriving at the Ministry of Fortune in growing numbers, reporting that along the back paths of the western outskirts, shadowy figures have been seen lingering. Those who claim to have spoken with them describe shifty replies and evasive eyes — hardly the bearing of decent folk. It is feared some mischief is being plotted in secret.\nThough Huaxu stands under the protection of the Nine Chapters of Dafeng, Drifters have long been said to lurk even within Haojing, committing offence upon offence. A censer that had burned unbroken for a hundred years was once extinguished — doused with water by the most notorious of their lot. Another drove a mechapuppet straight into the Hall of Adjudication. Others have hung wenzhu fronds outside people's doors to mark their targets, then ambushed them and left them bleeding. Their crimes are many; their wickedness unrepentant.\nEven so, there are always those who make excuses for them, shielding them in the dark and dressing them up with fine names like the Four Drifters. Fortunately, the Magistrates of Yingtian took action, and the so-called Four Drifters were seized and put to death.\nYet though their ringleaders and close followers have been dealt with, remnants are said to remain. The people fear renewed Drifter activity may flare again and bring fresh calamity.\nThe Hall of Adjudication has therefore issued the following order: the Magistrates' patrols are to be strengthened at once. A reward of 12,000 is hereby offered for any report of Drifters' whereabouts that is verified. Residents are further warned: do not enter desolate places after nightfall. Every measure is to be taken to root out the Drifters.\n...\n...\n...\n (The latter half of the sheet is torn; the remaining text is illegible.)",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000115"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000116 = {
          ContentEN = "On the surface of Haojing Lake, mist sometimes gathers into shapes — towers, palaces, immortals, spectres — each outlined with startling clarity. People call such sights the Mirage. Some say it is Lord Argent's breath; others insist it is the work of Lord Argent's eight immortal attendants. Accounts multiply without end. I doubt them all.\n\nI chased down every rumour I could find, yet reached no firm conclusion. I went about the city seeking out the elders, asking how one might reach the Mirage, and I began with the boatmen, hoping they would row me across the lake in search of it. They mocked my folly — yet they agreed readily once paid, and accompanied me on several trips. Back and forth we went, again and again, and still we found nothing.\n\nI searched daily until my meagre means were spent. A few, taking pity, would join me in their spare hours, though their earlier enthusiasm was gone. I did not resent them; I felt only shame. In the end I put what little remained to my name into buying a bamboo raft, resolved to seek the Mirage on my own.\n\nThough I am but a bookish sort, I grew up climbing hills and swimming rivers with the neighbourhood boys. I am no weakling — yet my skills go no further than that. At first, poling the raft was awkward and stiff; with time I grew steadily more practised.\n\nThe search proved arduous indeed. At first I tried to take my bearings and fix a heading. Later there was only the endless expanse, drifting and bobbing with the waves. Still I never beheld the Mirage's true face. In time my spirit faltered. I began to suspect I was clinging to emptiness, wasting my days and wearing down my resolve. Now and then I would glimpse it in the distance and strain towards it, only for it to vanish the moment I drew near — like moonlight on water, dissolving into nothing at the slightest touch.\n\nOne day a thick fog rolled in over Haojing, and I could not make out a person two steps away. Everyone warned me not to go, fearing I would lose my way on the lake and die there. I sighed and said, \"Other than my life, what do I have left to lose?\" My purse was already empty. We scraped by on my wife's needlework. Thankfully we had no children; otherwise, what kind of husband would that make me?\n\nStill I insisted on leaving. My wife wept and tried to stop me. \"If you step out that door,\" she said, \"I will never be your wife again.\" My resolve faltered — yet in the end I steeled myself, flung the door wide, and went.\n\nThe fog blotted out heaven and earth. When the tip of my pole struck the water, I could not even see where it fell. I could only feel the raft's motion through my wrists. The cold was sharper than on any ordinary day; it cut to the bone. Frost gathered in my beard; I breathed it away, and moments later it formed again.\n\nNothing about the scene seemed especially strange. Perhaps there was something wondrous, yet my mind was so muddled that I could not recall it. I remember only one instant: at one point it felt as though my pole was no longer meeting water at all, but something lighter — like pushing through cloud. A feeling too surreal for words to depict.\n\nI cannot say how long I went on. Then the fog began to thin. Before me, overlapping haloes of light shimmered, and within them rose halls and pavilions, radiant beyond measure. Joy seized me like a fever. I gathered what strength remained and drove the raft forward. Just as my strength drained, the raft struck shore. It was as though a stone had finally dropped from my chest. My heart, at last, began to steady.\n\nI left the raft behind and stepped ashore. The ground beneath my feet felt soft as water, yet I neither sank nor grew wet. With each step, ripples spread outward. It was wonder that defied belief.\n\n...",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000116"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000117 = {
          ContentEN = "Blessed be! You who have weathered such trials have at last reached this place. The grief I have borne through long years may now be laid bare beneath the open sky.\nIn the Battle of Shuli, both Jishu and Xuanheng suffered losses beyond count. Since the founding of Huaxu, no calamity so great had befallen the realm. Heaven wept blood; the mountains donned mourning white. Even the Nine Chapters of Dafeng recast their ordinances, taking on a sterner temper.\nAs to the cause, the world fixes blame upon Jishu, saying we lit the fuse and reaped our due.\nSuch is hindsight's judgement — and false besides.\nI yet recall: amid the tumult, our commander lay mortally wounded. With his final breath, he passed me a secret word — \"Jishu never sought this war. That day, heavy fog veiled the field; by ill chance, a war-automaton slipped its control. Startled, each host took it for the spark of old enmity, and blades were bared in blind dread. Each, fearing annihilation at the other's hand, fought at full measure. None could halt the clash, and thus the calamity came to pass.\"\nYet were such a knotted truth told abroad, who would credit it? The rift between Jishu and Xuanheng had sunk too deep to mend; to speak would not clear our intent — only hasten my own death. Thus I kept silence and buried this secret, awaiting one who might come after.\nThis half of the Jade of Equilibrium was once a sacred token of Jishu. Knowing its power was spent, yet unwilling to see it fall into Xuanheng hands, I hid it here.\nThe <H>Heavenwrought Cannon</> — the masterwork of my life's endeavour — lies at rest beyond the gates of the Labyrinth of Yanyin. Take the Yan Key before this stele, and wield it with wisdom.\nWe, who have withdrawn from the world in regret, shall not live to see the two sects reconciled. Yet I pray you will inherit this charge: mend the rift among men, and restore the concord of elder days. This is my solemn plea.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000117"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000118 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Meals Provided at Bloomfield Station

Breakfast: black bread, water
Lunch: boiled potatoes, bean soup
Dinner: no additional meal provided. Workers assigned to overnight duty may collect one portion of pickled vegetables from the gatehouse.

Notice

1. Under the Long Schedule, breakfast is distributed from 8:30 to 9:00. Under the Short Schedule, breakfast is distributed from 9:00 to 9:30. Lunch is served from 12:00 to 13:00 under both schedules. All times are determined by station clocks. Workers who report for duty after 6:00 are not entitled to meals that day.
2. Meal collection closes five minutes before the end of the meal period. Workers arriving after that time will not be served. When the work bell sounds, the canteen gates will be closed. Any worker who fails to return to their post on time will receive one demerit. Three demerits will result in dismissal.
3. Food may not be taken out of the canteen. Eating while on duty is prohibited. Unfinished food may not be taken back to the workplace.
4. Rejoining the queue to collect additional food is prohibited. Offenders will be charged that day's meal fee.
5. Child labourers may not eat while seated. Part-time workers are not authorised to enter the canteen.
6. Disruption of order in the canteen is prohibited, including, but not limited to: chatting, fainting, vomiting, quarrelling, queue-jumping, and breastfeeding.
7. Meal periods are determined by the bells. When the warning bell sounds, workers must begin leaving the canteen. When the work bell sounds, all workers must already be at their assigned posts for attendance inspection by the foreman.
8. Workers may not begin eating until food distribution has ended. Note: once the warning bell sounds, workers must stop eating, return their trays and utensils, and clean their places before leaving.
9. Workers may bring their own food, provided it is consumed in the canteen during the designated meal period. All food must be registered before entry into the canteen. In the event of diarrhoea or similar symptoms, the Station accepts no responsibility. Anyone who sees another worker bring in unregistered food may report them to the guard on duty.

...

(I thought this was just an ordinary workers' menu, but there's barely anything about the food. Most of the poster is taken up by cruel, pointless rules... Working conditions at Bloomfield Station must be appalling.)]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000118"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000119 = {
          ContentEN = "Linking the far south to the distant north, bridging distance and dreams — our criss-crossing railways span the land, and our stations shine like stars across the vast expanse of Hyperborea. Loyal subjects of His Majesty, let us stand as one, march forward hand in hand, and forge the future of the Hyperborean railways with our own hands!\nBloomfield Station warmly welcomes all who meet the following requirements:\n1. Hardworking, dependable, disciplined, of good character, and eager to build a better future\n2. No restrictions on age, gender, race, or previous work experience\n\nWhat we offer:\n1. Guidance from veteran workers with more than twenty years of experience. Whatever you wish to learn, you can learn here.\n2. A most stable wage, paid in full and never late.\n3. Daily meals provided on schedule, so every hardworking labourer is kept well fed.\n4. A united and friendly workplace. Here, rank and race are left at the door. From the moment you arrive, you are one of us. We welcome every citizen loyal to Hyperborea.\n\nJoin Bloomfield Station and begin a new chapter in your life. Even if you cannot fight for His Majesty's glory on the battlefield, you may still prove your worth here. Bloomfield Station needs you. So too does the future of Hyperborea.\nJoin us! Join the glorious cause!\n\n(What a stirring piece of recruitment propaganda... Anyone reading it would surely be swept up in its fervour. The terms it promises even sound rather appealing.)",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000119"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000120 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Today I am going to write my will
I still dont really know what a will is. I heard the fine lords saying it when they walked past. There was an old man dying and they said he had to write his will before he went. I didnt understand it much, but it sounded like something fancy. They all sounded happy too, like the old man dying and writing his will was a good thing. So before I die I ought to write a will too
I am probly not even old enough for that. At the ironworks they call the gate man that old bastard. Some people even call the stationmaster that when they got enough guts. But nobody ever called me old. They call me kid, oi you, or that Charon there
But that dont stop me dying. And it dont stop me writing a will neither
Only I still havent thought what to write. I never had much schooling. I dont know how long I was in school for. Not long I guess. I got sent out to work soon enough. There was no other way. My ma was dying then and she needed money so I stopped going to school and went to work
But she died in the end anyway
So thats me, a useless Karon. I am bad at working and bad at talking when people talk to me. I think maybe I am no use at all... No, not no use. I am good for getting yelled at. Maybe people feel better after they yell at me. My dad does. When he drinks he yells at me, then he hits me, then the next day he says sory to me. Sometimes I think he does it on purpose. He just wants to hit somebody and nobody else will let him
I keep thinking if anyone will be sad after I die. I guess my dad will. He wont have anyone to hit after that. I need a good way to die. One where there is still some money after. But I havent thought of one yet. I hope my dying can at least buy my dad a bottle of wine, or else I really am too useless. I wish I could die like that old man the fine lords were talking about. They all sounded so happy, like the old man dying was a good thing
Alright. I went off again. I have to think of a good way to die. But I still havent thought of one. Maybe if I wait a bit I wont want to die any more
But its so f***ing funny. I wrote all this and I still cant think of one thing I want to live for... Forget it then. I will just die
Alright that is all for my will. I dont know what else to write. If I could get that old mans will maybe I could copy it and make mine look better. But I cant get it obviously. Anyway that is all. I am going to think how to do it now]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000120"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000121 = {
          ContentEN = "1. When addressing His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn, members must use the proper honorific in full.\n(All hail Stationmaster Eisenbahn!)\n(That right there is a textbook mistake. The full form is \"His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn\"! Not a single word may be omitted!)\n\n2. All attention must remain on His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn. Members are not to bring up other public figures, including opera stars, stage actors, and the like.\n(*a name scratched out* has been an embarrassment lately. How can someone act that badly and still call themselves a professional? Unlike His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn — he never lets us down.)\n(Didn't we have a rule about not mentioning other public figures? Why is this one still breaking the rules? Don't let me find out who wrote this.)\n\n3. Memorise His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn's birthday, height, body measurements, and weight.\n(Latest update — His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn, has lost another kilo. Current weight: 73 kg.)\n(He's lost weight again from working so hard... Breaks my heart. What are the people around him even doing? How did no one notice?)\n(Working hard? Entertaining foreign businesswomen, more like!)\n(Found the anti. Get out. This handbook is for Salon members only.)\n(Lol. People are actually spreading romance rumours about His Honour? Everyone knows there's room in his heart only for work.)\n\n4. Members of the Steamwhistle Salon must support one another and must not hoard memorabilia connected to His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn, including cigarette cases, posters, and similar items.\n\n5. The sale of unauthorised merchandise featuring His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn, is strictly forbidden. Offenders will bear all consequences.\n\n...\n\n54. Members must actively safeguard the reputation of His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn, and firmly oppose all attempts to belittle him, smear his name, slander him, or spread malicious rumours. If necessary, extreme measures may be employed.\n\n(So... throwing bricks is allowed?)\n(You could, but that's terribly ungentlemanly. I have connections at the Security Bureau — write the name here and I'll take care of them.)\n\n(I'm new... there are way too many rules. If I can't memorise them all, can I write cheat notes?)\n(How can you fail to remember this little? What are you, a plant from a rival fan club?)",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000121"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000122 = {
          ContentEN = "Daily Activity Report: His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn\n\n12 September, Clear — Bloomfield Station Freight Yard\nApprox. 10:00 — Subject observed in the mid-rear section of the freight yard. Accompanied by four guards. Engaged in conversation with officials from Icelake. Subject repeatedly directed his gaze into the interiors of freight carriages.\n\n25 September, Overcast — Station Concourse\n15:00 — Subject appeared at the ticket gate in disguise. Wearing a checked cap with a long peak and brown-tinted sunglasses. Behaviour deemed suspicious.\n\n2 October, Cloudy, Clearing Later — Bloomfield Station Freight Yard\n01:40 — An unauthorised train entered the station. Subject not sighted. Members of his personal guard were present. Incident confirmed to be closely connected to the meeting on 12 September.\n\n5 October, Rain — Bloomfield Station Materials Yard\nSubject not sighted. An intoxicated merchant was observed loitering in the area, alleging deception and betrayal in connection with a transaction. The individual later left the station. Violent assault suspected thereafter.\n\n27 October, Clear — Platform\nSubject appeared in full military uniform. Offered formal greetings to boarding passengers and remained on site to see the train off. Presentation stable. No abnormalities observed.\n\n...",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000122"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000123 = {
          ContentEN = [[
In a neat hand: Looking to buy His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn's 2nd Anniversary Poster. 50 silver.
In a hasty hand: That little for a 2nd Anniversary? You couldn't even get this year's poster for that.
In a cramped, cursive hand: 80 silver. Deal or no deal?
In a neat hand: Sorry... that's more than I'm willing to pay.

In a broken, uneven hand: Anyone want His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn's Birthday Commemorative Cigarettes? No cigarette pack, just the cigarettes.
In a neat hand: What's the point of buying just the cigarettes?
In a broken, uneven hand: You can smoke the same kind as His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn.

In a bold, forceful hand: His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn's 5th Anniversary Commemorative Stamp, rubbing edition. 50 silver. Trade in person tomorrow at 15:00.
In a childish hand: "Rubbing edition"? Yeah, right. Isn't that just a knock-off you made yourself?
In a neat hand: Do not defile the Sacred Ledger. Keep this nonsense out of it!

...
In an elegant hand: His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn will appear on the platform tomorrow afternoon. Further details available by private trade.

In a light, rounded hand: His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn's 10th Anniversary Photography Event. Looking for a partner. I'll bring the camera; you take the photo. I'm a terrible photographer.

In a sharp, angular hand: His Honour, Stationmaster Eisenbahn's newcomer welcome event souvenir available. Leave contact details if interested.
In a childish hand: Where did you even get those? The chairman said no reselling. Consider this a warning.]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000123"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000124 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Chapter 189: The Rails Will Stretch Ever Farther

"Stationmaster Leo, is there truly no way to increase this station's capacity fivefold?"
Leo set down the Minister of Transport's letter and let out a quiet sigh.
It was not the minister's seemingly unreasonable demand that weighed on him. Leo knew all too well that, with the front lines mired in bitter fighting, a fivefold increase was not merely reasonable. It was necessary.
The railways were the veins of Hyperborea. Through them flowed supplies, weapons, equipment, and legionaries bound for every corner of the Empire, carrying hot lifeblood through its body without pause.
As stationmaster, Leo knew better than anyone that the Empire's current rail capacity was nowhere near enough to meet the needs of the front. Without a dramatic increase in efficiency, the soldiers fighting there might soon find themselves short of guns and ammunition, forced to charge the enemy with nothing but their fists.
Hyperborean soldiers would remain brave and unyielding even then. But Leo, entrusted with the Empire's transport lifeline, knew it was his duty to do everything in his power to spare his countrymen at the front needless sacrifice.
He picked up his communicator, his mind already racing through ways to improve the station's efficiency.
"Expand the station... centralise dispatch... refit passenger trains for military freight..."
He opened a channel and began assigning tasks to his subordinates according to their strengths.
"Kate, you have the contacts. Gather every available hand you can find. I'll bring in engineers from the Aeolipile, and together we'll lay the new tracks."
"Understood."
"Wolfe, you know machinery. Put together an emergency repair crew. If a train breaks down, I want it dragged off the track and repaired immediately."
"Understood."
...
Once all the tasks had been assigned, Leo rolled a cigarette and gazed south, towards the Elysian Church.
"One day, I will see the Empire's railways laid all the way to the Tower of Elysium. I will see the Empire's veins reach every last inch of the false god's lands."
Leo tucked the cigarette away, turned, and strode back towards the station. A fierce wind swept past, snapping his greatcoat behind him, but his stride never faltered.
"I'll save this cigarette for that day."

Can Leo carry out his superiors' order? Can his station bear the burden of sustaining the front? The saga continues in the next instalment of The Invincible Stationmaster.]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000124"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000125 = {
          ContentEN = "Request Ⅰ\nRoutine Maintenance of Bloomfield Station\nBudget: 100,000 gold coins\nDecision: Denied\nNotes: Last year's maintenance budget was 80,000 gold coins. Some increase is justified given the station's age, but this is still far too much. Keep your spending under control. Some parts now cost more to maintain than to replace. I have a list here — inspect them accordingly.\n\nRequest Ⅱ\nThe Alchemy Academy has requested funding from the station for the development of its newest locomotive. If successful, it will be put into service at Bloomfield Station first.\nBudget: 245,700 gold coins\nDecision: Reduced budget approved — 45,700 gold coins\nNotes: All those odd figures make it seem convincing, don't they? They requested sponsorship from us under the same pretext a few years ago. Then last year, when I visited the stationmaster in Dismania, I learned they had made the same promise to every station of any real size. They are simply trying to squeeze money out of us. Still, we cannot afford an open confrontation with them. Give them a token sum and close the matter.\n\nRequest Ⅲ\nThe Steamwhistle Salon requests funding for activities promoting Stationmaster Eisenbahn's public image.\nBudget: 10,000 gold coins\nDecision: Denied\nNotes: This organisation has nothing to do with me. If they submit another request, do not bring it to my desk. Reject it outright.\n\nRequest Ⅳ\nImprovement of labour conditions for station workers\nBudget: 10,000 gold coins\nDecision: Increased to 30,000 gold coins\nDirective: Redirect the amount cut from routine maintenance to this project. Proceed under the usual arrangements. Full authorisation granted.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000125"
        },
        READ_TEXT_2000126 = {
          ContentEN = "The incident occurred at 09:00 yesterday morning. Princess Flodia departed from Platform Ⅰ aboard the Jackdaw, bound for the Imperial Capital, Dismania.\nOne of the perpetrators, an Icelake Phoxhunter whose identity has yet to be confirmed, had boarded the Gale at 08:00, also bound for Dismania. Roughly three hours after departure, the Jackdaw overtook the Gale. The Phoxhunter seized the opportunity to leap aboard the Jackdaw. Taking advantage of the relatively light guard presence on the roof, they entered the carriage from above, seized Princess Flodia, and jumped with her onto the Aurora Express, another train travelling on an adjacent track.\n\nThe Aurora Express was then hijacked by Katya of the Hazering, one of the leaders of the Forsakens, known by the codename \"Beast-King\". After the hijacking began, the 6th Legion stationed in the Cocytus Region immediately dispatched the airship Deepwinter in response. Katya used Princess Flodia as a hostage to prevent the airship from engaging. She later used the Aurora Express's main cannon to strike Deepwinter, and the blast also ignited a Heaventree in southern Cocytus.\nA violent confrontation then broke out between Katya and the Phoxhunter, presumably over what was to be done with Princess Flodia. Katya was later extracted by members of the Forsakens. The Phoxhunter's whereabouts remain unknown, and they are presumed to have been killed in the locomotive explosion.\n\nThe incident caused significant losses. The airship Deepwinter crashed, and the Aurora Express's locomotive was wrecked beyond repair.\nIn addition, dense airborne particulates released by the burning Heaventree will render the airspace between Cocytus and Dismania impassable for a considerable period. Should the Forsakens launch a large-scale uprising during this time, the Empire will have difficulty redeploying airships from across its territory to suppress the unrest.\nPrincess Flodia's current whereabouts are unknown. However, according to passengers aboard the Aurora Express who saw her during the incident, she does not appear to have been caught in the locomotive explosion.\n\nThe incident resulted in the deaths of more than a dozen civilians, with over forty others injured. The conductor, driver, and many train guards were killed in action while resisting the Forsakens. We honour these brave souls. The Empire will see the demons of the Forsakens pay blood for blood.\nAll glory to our Emperor!\n\nAttachment: Civilian Victims, 13 Total\nJeff Eysen — Icelake courier\nElena Horn — Icelake fur merchant\nCliff Miller — Dismania bank clerk\n...",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_2000126"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20602 = {
          ContentEN = "January 1st\nToday, ▇▇▇▇ gave me some Phoxene shards as \"pocket money.\" She said that back in the capital, kids get pocket money from their elders before they come of age — to spend on treats or toys.\nThere aren't any shops around here, so she told me to save it. Then, when there's something I really want or need, I can use it to ask for her help.\nThing is... I can't really think of anything I want.\n\n\nJanuary 2nd\nI've got it! I could get {nickname} a \"birthday present\"! Maybe one of those \"birthday cakes\" kids in the capital have when they turn a year older — I read they're really tasty! They might be expensive... but if I save enough pocket money to give to ▇▇▇▇, I bet she could help me get one.\nI found an old clay jar in the kitchen. It's just right for keeping my savings.\n\n\nJanuary 16th\nWe were playing hide-and-seek today, and {nickname} found the jar and almost opened it... I need to find a better hiding spot.\nMaybe tomorrow I'll sneak into the room where ▇▇▇▇ does her alchemy work. There are loads of strange machines in there — she never lets {nickname} go near them.\n\n\nJanuary 17th\nI hid my piggy bank under the <H>waterfall, right next to the alchemy chamber</>. That way, neither {nickname} nor ▇▇▇▇ will stumble across it.\n\n\n...\n\n\n▇▇ ▇▇\n{nickname}'s birthday is coming up, and I've saved a whole box full of pocket money. It should be enough for a cake. But ▇▇▇▇ seems really busy these days. She's been staying up late a lot... Maybe it's not the right time to bother her. I guess I'll ask her next year.\nWith another year of saving, I can get an even bigger cake — big enough for all three of us.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20602"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20603 = {
          ContentEN = "Dear Berry,\n \n If you are reading this letter, it means you've returned after all.\n You've always been a good girl, and I trust you had your reasons for coming back.\n I've left some supplies for you, buried <H>near the airship</> — they should be enough to get you both off Purgatorio Island.\n I wish you the best on your journey. Stay safe.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20603"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20604 = {
          ContentEN = "The Goddess of Fate weaves the stars into their paths, gifting us with guiding lights that lead us towards eternal bliss.\n Yet, in our times, humanity has strayed from Her teachings. We have forsaken the bonds of love and compassion, choosing instead to arm ourselves with cold steel against our own kin. Our sacred lands have crumbled, and even Her sanctuaries have been defiled.\n Should this be the ordeal She has devised — a sombre prelude to the dawn — then to you, the destined one who will one day reach this place, I offer a blessing that transcends time.\n \n In <H>a place overlooked by Her gaze</>, I have hidden the final legacy of an age when humanity cherished love and solidarity.\n May Her tender threads of fate lead you to the true dawn.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20604"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20605 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Time, slips swiftly by, ever a fading treasure.
In the distant corners of memory, truth stays hidden.
Having pierced countless illusions, the path leads under.
Through winding stone, with divine light, to the forgotten cave.
In lands devoid of life and hope, a new cycle takes root.]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20605"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20608 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Sorry, babe, there's been a hiccup at home, so I can't take you to the inner city this month as I promised. But hang tight, I'll come for you as soon as I sort things out. Trust me one more time, okay?
 As a little something to make it up to you, I've hidden some cash <H>under the grass on the left side of the pathway</> to the inner city. Don't forget to pick it up. Love you!
 
 <W>You liar! You jerk! Every time you lie to me, you just throw money at me like I'm some kind of pet you own! I'd rather starve or drop dead in the street than take another coin from you! You can rot with your filthy money for all I care!</>]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20608"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20609 = {
          ContentEN = "Hey there! Are you tired of struggling to make ends meet?\nSick of breaking your back every day for a pittance, only to be treated like dirt?\nDo you feel like, no matter how hard you try, you just can't catch a break?\nDon't beat yourself up — it's not your fault. What you're lacking isn't skill, but a stroke of luck! All you need is the right opportunity, and you could be the next bigshot!\nAnd guess what? It's your lucky day! Just last month, the chief engineer of our Legendary Mining Alliance unveiled a new mining marvel, ten years in the making! It is user-friendly, boasts an impressive battery life, and the cherry on top — it can automatically extract microscopic Phoxene particles — invisible to the naked eye — and turn them into solid Phoxene! With this revolutionary technology, we can breathe new life into the exhausted mines and bring Icelake's mining industry back from the brink!\n \nWe're on the lookout for partners right now! Opportunities like this come once in a lifetime!\nWant in? Solve the puzzle below for a chance to join our elite team!\n \n(Beneath the text is a simple map of the slums with a Phoxene symbol marking a spot — it appears to be near <H>a pile of old junk by the roadside rubbish bin</>.)",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20609"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20610 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Someone found my secret stash last time...
This time, I consulted a professional, who suggested I hide it somewhere outside the city, perhaps <H>somewhere near a puddle</>.
Sure, Phoxene won't go bad in water, but what if the box gets mouldy or rots from the moisture? Then my stash would literally be "money down the drain", right?
Still, it's better than having it stolen... I'm going to give it a shot. I'll check on it next week.]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20610"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20611 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Hey, wanna play a game?
If you can find the <H>biggest gem</> around here, I'll give you a reward!]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20611"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20612 = {
          ContentEN = [[
<W>There are no 100 pieces of Phoxene here.</>
 
(Beneath this message, some squiggly lines attempt to outline the mine's paths. The spot marked with a red circle appears to be <H>near the mine's entrance</>.)]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20612"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20613 = {
          ContentEN = "I saw it with my own eyes — blue light, shining out from <H>under that fallen pillar</>! But there's no lighting rig down there... none at all. There's definitely something weird going on!\nLike... maybe some miner worked himself to death and just... never left. No wonder no one's bothered to fix it since.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20613"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20614 = {
          ContentEN = "Hello, stranger, I am an explorer.\nThis cavern is absolutely enchanting, like something out of a fairy tale where snow fairies might live! The massive, crystal-clear ice pillars, the rugged old roots of a withered Heaventree, and the complex terrain that twists and turns like a natural labyrinth... it'll take your breath away!\nIf you've made it this far, don't give up now — the scenery up there is even more spectacular! I've left a gift and some provisions for you at the <H>cavern's summit</>. Keep going — you're almost there.\nMay you discover the same thrill of adventure that drives me. As long as there's life in you, let the journey never end!",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20614"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20615 = {
          ContentEN = "Do you remember that day we left the city and wandered along the <H>lakeside of Lamenting Lake</>, wading barefoot through the shallows?\n\"Look at that tree with red leaves,\" you said.\n\"They're orange,\" I corrected.\n\"I said red,\" you insisted.\n\nI'd saved up a few pieces of Phoxene — I was going to trade them for a real red-leafed plant, as a gift for you.\nIn the end, I buried them <H>beneath that tree with the orange leaves</>.\n\n...Yes, I still think they're orange. I'm sorry. Leaves don't turn red just because you say they are — just like you won't come back just because I keep saying you will.\n\nMaybe, when spring comes, the Phoxene will sprout and grow — like the feelings I still carry for you. Or maybe some lucky passerby will dig them up instead.\n\nEither way, I think that would be a good thing. You always said good things are meant to be shared. If this gift I never gave ends up helping someone else... maybe that's a kind of happy ending, too.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20615"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20616 = {
          ContentEN = "Fact: if you keep an egg somewhere warm, it hatches into a chick.\nSo if I leave some Phoxene somewhere warm... they'll grow into a Heaventree, right? Then I'll be rich. Forever.\nI'm a genius! No, scratch that — I'm a true visionary!\n\nAnd guess what? I'm on <H>furnace</> maintenance duty. Hehehe... Tomorrow, I'll sneak my Phoxene in and start the hatching...",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20616"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20617 = {
          ContentEN = "Everyone's got Snowcaps all wrong! They're not dangerous — they're delicious! Simmer them just right, season with care, and boom — gourmet magic, totally safe!\nBut that's old news. I'm done with boring, basic mushrooms. I want more. Something wild. Something weird.\nI need the king of mushrooms!\n\nSo I scoured the sewers all day, and finally, <H>near the deepest sluice gate</>, I found them — a whole cluster of brand-new mushrooms! Just the colours were enough to make my mouth water. I took a few back. Already scribbling down some ideas for the first recipe draft...\n\nThey say people used to leave offerings to the gods for good harvests. So maybe... if I leave something under this mushroom patch, the great Mushroom God of Atlasia will reward me with even tastier treasures? Didn't have much on me — just a handful of coins and a few snacks. Gave it all. Hope the Mushroom God's not too picky...",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20617"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20620 = {
          ContentEN = "The fortress won't hold much longer... Don't be a fool and stay behind just because of orders. You're not even on the official roster — no one will notice if you're gone.\nThere are spare supplies <H>near the airship</>. Take what you need and head home!\n\n(The bottom half of the page is unreadable from dark red splodges.)",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20620"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20621 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Temporary Supply Pick-up Point:

<H>Beneath the control console by the viewing window on the engine room's outer perimeter.</>]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20621"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20622 = {
          ContentEN = "Hey, my dear ex-teammates — let's celebrate a little festival of our own!\n \nNow, don't be hasty to shoot me down. I know the Fortress only allows Triumph Day... but surely there's no harm in a secret celebration or two, right?\nBack home, we mark the first of April with the Snowmelt Festival: trinkets hung from pine boughs as the snow retreats, gifts exchanged beneath the trees, and a fond farewell to winter as spring draws near.\nYou all remember that pine in the <H>Simulated Battlefield</>, don't you? The one we riddled with bullet holes and cordite burns during drills? If you squint, I'd say those count as decorations. Next week just happens to be your training rotation — and I may have buried a little something <H>beneath that tree by the water</>. Volunteer for post-training clean-up and \"accidentally\" dig them up, yeah~?\n(One each — no hoarding! I'll be watching, 0^0)\n \nEven though I've been transferred to another squad, we'll always be best buds!",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20622"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20630 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Ughh... A few days ago, trying to show off in front of my friends, I climbed all the way up onto the head of the Bi'an statue <H>behind the Admonition Stele</>. Now I've come down with a cold and taken a nasty fall. Feels like punishment catching up with me...
I've left all of this month's pocket money at <H>Lord Bi'an's feet</>. Really hope Lord Bi'an won't hold it against me...]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20630"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20631 = {
          ContentEN = "...\n\n21 September\nI felt like trying my luck today, so I opened five Lucky Eggs. The first four were nothing special: a box of toothpicks, a rag, a packet of Skylily seeds, and some throat lozenges. But the last one actually turned out to be a packet of fine Mistwharf Green Tea!\n Lunocarp said my luck was pretty good... could I really be Lady Luck's favourite after all!?\n Might try a few more when I get the chance.\n\n...\n\n27 September\nOpened ten Lucky Eggs today. Got three sachets, a voucher for osmanthus cakes, two discount coupons that each take seven silver off a better theatre seat (and they don't even stack!), a wooden brush, a hundred grams of dried peafowl droppings — the note says it's medicinal, but it's honestly just disgusting, so I'll probably bury it in the field when I get home — plus a block of ink and a box of matches.\n Darn it... not a single rare item these past few days! If it weren't for how cute Lunocarp is, I really wouldn't bother opening another one.\n\n28 September\nLunocarp talked me into opening more than twenty Lucky Eggs today. She said I'd hit the guarantee and could get a \"super rare\" one. And what was inside? Two slips of paper, pet food, and some kid stuff!!!\nWhat a rip-off! Why doesn't Bi'an step in and do something about it!?\nI'm done. I'm never opening another Lucky Egg again!\n\nIn a minute I'm throwing the rubbish straight into the <H>lotus pond</> to feed the fish!",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20631"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20632 = {
          ContentEN = "I am but an old wanderer of the Wuqiongzang Sect. By chance I came upon this quiet, hidden place and made it my training ground.\nNow, having lived more than seventy years, it is time for these old bones to retire from the martial world. You who have found this note — to reach this place, you must possess uncommon aptitude and no small measure of courage.\nI have buried a treasured martial manual <H>beneath the apricot tree in the rear court of the Hall of Ephemerality</>.\nFollow its teachings, and within half a month you will cross water without leaving a trace; train for a further half-month, and a thousand miles will be but a single day's journey to you.\n\nI leave this manual to you now. May it carry you swiftly towards the peak of your art.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20632"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20633 = {
          ContentEN = "Ever since our debate that day, one thing has become clear to me: if we stay in Haze Market, we will never know what freedom truly is, nor what it costs.\n\nWhat we do here — all the little tricks and the loopholes we exploit — are merely ways of bending the rules without ever going beyond them. I don't want freedom that lives on compromise. There must be a freedom in this world that needs no cunning and no pretence... and I want to prove it exists. So I have to go — beyond the mountain we've looked to so many times, and further still, to heights neither of us has yet seen. Even if it means becoming a wanderer, cut loose from the world.\n\nBy the time you read this, I will be far away. I've left a few gifts <H>on the old boat by the water</> where we always played. Forgive me for leaving without a proper farewell, but I promise you this: we will meet again, on the day I find my answer.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20633"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20634 = {
          ContentEN = "I'd heard the Gritblight Expanse was brutally dangerous, and after today's wandering... well, it really is — terrifying at times, but thrilling all the same. Look closely and the place even has a strange sort of beauty to it; if not for the Filthoids running wild, it might pass for a scenic spot.\n\nBut with nothing around me except rock and deadwood, it's far too easy to lose your bearings. I left a pack behind earlier when I stopped to rest — I'm fairly sure it was in some <H>cave</>... but after circling the area for ages, I still can't find the entrance. At this rate, night will catch me out here.\n\nForget it. Whoever comes across it can have it...",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20634"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20640 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Cassia and sandalwood breathe their fragrance free;
orchid and musk sweeten the breeze.
Ask where the hidden treasure may be,
and the shepherd boy points to the golden tree.]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20640"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20641 = {
          ContentEN = [[
East of the city, where wild grasses creep,
lies a cavern with pathways running deep.
Night after night, searching bears no fruit;
yet the treasure lies where the grasses root.]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20641"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20642 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Southward, northward, east and west,
seekers roam each road in restless quest.
But where, at last, will the treasure be?
Above the water, beneath the dead tree.]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20642"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20650 = {
          ContentEN = "Thanks for covering for me the other day. Who'd have thought the foreman would show up right when we were having a quick game? Good job you're quick. Just... next time, come up with a better excuse, will you? \"Running to the loo\" is fine, but all that about... messy trousers? Bit much, mate. Anyway, cheers for that. I finally got a decent hand today — would've been gutted if the foreman had dragged me back. Your share of the winnings is <H>by the stove</>. Don't forget to grab it.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20650"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20651 = {
          ContentEN = "Do you want to know the secret to an easier day's work? It's simple — make regular offerings to the ancient god of the rails of Atlasia! Just leave your offerings <H>by the tracks</>, and the great god will hear our prayers! The breakdown rate of the train I look after has dropped a lot recently — all thanks to this almighty god!",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20651"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20652 = {
          ContentEN = "This is awful... When I got off the train, I noticed a passenger's wallet lying on the floor. For a moment, I lost my head and quietly took it. But when I opened it, it was full of strange papers that looked like charms, covered in drawings of black moons. Absolutely creepy. I heard those are the symbol of some kind of cult from Elysian Church or something?\nI've left the wallet <H>by the infirmary door</>. Hopefully, that'll help someone in need and make up for what I did. The Great Seven of Elysian Church? Or was it nine? Whichever it is, please don't curse me — I didn't take the money! I beg you!",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20652"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20653 = {
          ContentEN = "(From the creases, this appears to have once been a carefully written letter, but now it lies unfolded among the clutter by the wall, as if someone carelessly tossed it aside...)\n\nDear Miss <H>Broadcaster</>... I hope this is not too forward, but if I fail to convey the fervour of my heart to you, I would regret it for the rest of my life!\nThis is my first time in Icelake. The strict security at the station left me at a loss, and I unfortunately lost my way. Luckily, you appeared before me like an angel descending from heaven — even though most of you is hidden behind your uniform, I can still catch a glimpse of your pink hair, as radiant as spring blossoms, and your violet eyes, which have utterly enchanted me.\nAlthough you already pointed me toward the exit, I have decided to stay and wait for the special broadcast you mentioned, just to hear your lovely voice once more.\nI have left a small gift at your <H>workplace</> as a token of gratitude for your hard work. When your sweet voice fills the station, I will be in the hall, cheering louder than anyone for you!",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20653"
        },
        READ_TEXT_20654 = {
          ContentEN = "Tch, you’re always just squatting by the tracks watching trains. Your hair smells like coal dust now, you know that?\nGot some good news for you: I’ve hidden some of my pocket money under the trees nearby. Enough to buy two months' worth of your favourite serial. How about that? Reckon you’ll finally get off your backside and take a walk now? Use the chance to stretch those stiff legs. Trust me, you might actually find there’s more to life than locomotives. Nature’s not bad once you give it a go!",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_20654"
        },
        READ_TEXT_JailInves01 = {
          ContentEN = " ※ Oath of Allegiance\n\nSource: Testimony by Calem, soldier of the Narwhals \n According to Calem, his commanding officer, Falsi, retrieved this document from the central control room shortly before the explosion. \n It is a letter of defection, pledging allegiance to the Forsakens — signed by Avar Hrodland. \n  \n Hellfire's Note: \n Handwriting and fingerprints confirmed. \n Still — could it be forged? \n  \n  \n ※ Correspondence from Avar Hrodland \n This letter contains specific instructions addressed to {nickname2} and Theodore, detailing acts of disruption to be carried out during the assault.\n \n \n ※ Control Panel: Central Control Room \n Status: Data compromised due to blast damage. \n Recovered via classified recorder — access logs from the final three days: \n • 18 Nov: No entries\n • 19 Nov: No entries\n • 20 Nov: Avar Hrodland, {nickname2}\n  \n Hellfire's Note: \n Worth noting.\n \n \n ※ Audio Recorder \n Recovered from the hallway by Catto (Narwhals unit), allegedly dropped during the Forsakens' retreat. \n At timestamp 33:17, a voice resembling Avar Hrodland is heard in conversation with an unidentified Forsakens operative. \n  \n Hellfire's Note: \n May prove useful in establishing a timeline. Credibility remains in question.\n \n  \n ※ Testimony from Calem (Narwhals unit) \n Statements as follows:\n • \"I saw Captain {nickname2} of the Griffins exit the central control room alone — there was blood all over {性别2:his|her} chest.\"\n • \"{性别2:He|She} told us to hold position while {性别2:he|she} went to check the sewers for any remaining Forsakens personnel.\"\n • \"Two minutes later, the alarm sounded. Captain Falsi and I rushed in — found Captain Avar of the Snow Wolves lying in a pool of blood. The self-destruct sequence had already been triggered.\"\n  \n Hellfire's Note: \n Internal conflict?\n \n \n ※ Outstanding Questions\n • Why was the control room destroyed?\n → A cover-up to eliminate further evidence?\n \n Conflicting Accounts:\n → Testimonies from {nickname2} and Theodore directly contradict the findings above.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_JailInves01"
        },
        READ_TEXT_JailInves02 = {
          ContentEN = " ※ Investigation into the Family Background of Daphne Theodore\n\nThe Theodore family currently resides in the royal capital, Dismania. Several members serve at the Royal Alchemy Academy. Notably, both Marcus Theodore (Daphne's maternal grandfather) and Claudia Theodore (her mother) have been awarded the First-Class Emerald Order by the Aeolipile.\nNo criminal record has been found in connection with the Theodore family.\n \n Further inquiry reveals that Daphne's biological father is Avar Hrodland. Although Avar and Claudia officially divorced five years ago, Avar has continued to maintain contact with Daphne since.\n\n ※ Investigation into the Family Background of {nickname2}\n {nickname2} is a confirmed survivor of the Alexis airship crash, which occurred three years ago during a military operation against the Elysian Church. According to {nickname2}, {性别2:he|she} suffers from memory loss, though exhibits no signs of cognitive dysfunction.\nNotably, eyewitness accounts from other survivors confirm that {nickname2} was not listed among the original passengers on the Alexis. In other words, <H>{nickname2} was a bona fide \"ghost passenger.\"</>\nAt the time, the 13th Legion conducted a comprehensive investigation into {nickname2}'s background but was unable to verify {性别2:his|her} identity. However, the possibility of {性别2:him|her} being an Elysian Church spy was definitively ruled out. A leading theory suggests that {性别2:he|she} may have been a local civilian who sought shelter in the wreckage during the bombing and suffered a head injury, resulting in partial amnesia.\nPlaced under close observation by the 13th Legion, and with Prince Vergilius acting as guarantor, {nickname2} was granted admission to the Imperial Military College. {性别2:He|She} graduated with distinction. During this time, Avar Hrodland served as one of {性别2:his|her} instructors.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_JailInves02"
        },
        READ_TEXT_fushuyian01 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Info: Liu, female, twelve years of age. Consulted on the sixth day of the eleventh month.

Chief Complaint: Went out to play in the snow yesterday. Upon returning home, suddenly felt dizzy with a heavy head. By nightfall developed chills and fever, headache, aching limbs, absence of sweating, and occasional cough with thin, clear phlegm.

Four Diagnostic Methods: 
Observation: complexion pale; tongue with thin white coating.
Auscultation: voice somewhat heavy; cough slightly congested.
Inquiry: appetite fair; stool and urine normal; headaches preventing sleep at night.
Palpation: pulse floating and tight.

Pattern Identification: Cold binding the exterior, constraining the defensive qi, and inhibiting the diffusion of lung qi.

Treatment & Formula: Acrid-warm method to release the exterior, promote the diffusion of lung qi, and dispel cold.
Prescription: Ma Huang Decoction.]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_fushuyian01"
        },
        READ_TEXT_fushuyian02 = {
          ContentEN = [[
Info: Chen, female, thirty-one years of age. First consultation in the twelfth month.

Chief Complaint: Has studied late into the night for the past half-year. In recent months, vision has become blurred; eyes dry, sore, and distended, with tearing when exposed to wind. Occasional dizziness and tinnitus.

Four Diagnostic Methods: 
Observation: complexion pale, with dark periorbital shadows; tongue red, scant in fluids, with a thin white coating.
Auscultation: voice clear and bright; occasional dry cough.
Inquiry: studies until late at night before sleeping; seldom goes outdoors by day; often feels restless and easily fatigued.
Palpation: pulse fine and wiry.

Pattern Identification: Liver-blood deficiency failing to nourish the eyes, with concurrent yin deficiency and hyperactive fire.

Treatment & Formula: Method to nourish liver and blood, enrich yin, and brighten the eyes.
Prescription: Bright-Eye Decoction.]],
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_fushuyian02"
        },
        READ_TEXT_fushuyian03 = {
          ContentEN = "Info: Liu, male, forty years of age. Consulted in the third month of spring.\n\nChief Complaint: Wife died of illness four years ago. Since then has become increasingly withdrawn and unable to sleep at night. Often sits alone in his late wife's room, calling her name and speaking as if she were still by his side — sewing, or calling him to eat. By day, spirit dull and unfocused; appetite poor; body progressively emaciated.\n\nFour Diagnostic Methods: \nObservation: complexion sallow-pale; eyes vacant; hair dry and withered; tongue with thin, dry, white coating.\nAuscultation: voice low and faint; occasional murmuring, mostly words addressed to his late wife.\nInquiry: ...\nPalpation: ...\n\nPattern Identification: Longstanding grief causing emotional injury; liver qi constrained, and the heart deprived of nourishment, leaving the spirit restless and unsettled.\n\nTreatment & Formula: Method to nourish the heart and calm the spirit, soothe the liver and relieve constraint, and concurrently tonify qi and blood.\nPrescription: self-formulated decoction for calming the spirit.",
          TextMapId = "READ_TEXT_fushuyian03"
        },
        RESOURCE_DESC_100 = {
          ContentEN = [[
High-grade Phoxene derived from Phoxene Plumule.
Like precious metals, they function as common currency across Atlasia, accepted universally for trade.]],
          TextMapId = "RESOURCE_DESC_100"
        },
        RESOURCE_DESC_10001 = {
          ContentEN = "A weathered old bough, snapped by fierce winds, bearing the scars of erosion that seem to have sapped it of all life.",
          TextMapId = "RESOURCE_DESC_10001"
        },
        RESOURCE_DESC_10002 = {
          ContentEN = "Originating from the Hyperborean Empire, powered by Phoxichor, this apparatus generates tremendous gusts. On grand occasions, these artificial winds elevate petals, banners, and the very grandeur of the empire.",
          TextMapId = "RESOURCE_DESC_10002"
        },
        RESOURCE_DESC_10003 = {
          ContentEN = "A butterfly navigating the ocean through air currents. Even in still weather, it can glide across the ocean via the currents generated from flapping its delicate wings — a small marvel with the ability to command the winds.",
          TextMapId = "RESOURCE_DESC_10003"
        },
        RESOURCE_DESC_10004 = {
          ContentEN = "Originating from Huaxu, this colossal and weighty arrow defies even the fiercest storms. One can scarcely conceive who might wield such a bolt, yet its meteor-like trail across the heavens shall be indelibly etched in the memory of any witnesses.",
          TextMapId = "RESOURCE_DESC_10004"
        },
        RESOURCE_DESC_10005 = {
          ContentEN = "Scales from a desert lizard, specially structured to endure scorching heat without harm. This trait adds credence to the fabled kinship between lizards and dragons, though the latter, a creature of beauty, dwells only in the realms of myth and fantasy.",
          TextMapId = "RESOURCE_DESC_10005"
        },
        RESOURCE_DESC_10006 = {
          ContentEN = "The eternal flame of sanctity, a beacon signifying that Khemet endures as long as this fire burns. To the desert folk, offering fire is a gesture of everlasting friendship.",
          TextMapId = "RESOURCE_DESC_10006"
        },
        RESOURCE_DESC_10007 = {
          ContentEN = "Shackles of exceptional make, forged over ages and impervious to heat, crafted to bind even the wildest of captives.",
          TextMapId = "RESOURCE_DESC_10007"
        }
      }
    end
  },
